hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1201","1278 - Yahya ibn Isma'il Abu Zakariyya al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in Maghrib the short Mufassal."
"1202","1279 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi narrated to us, from al-Dahhak, from Bukayr, from Sulayman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I have not seen anyone more resembling the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than so-and-so. Bukayr said: I asked Sulayman - and he had met that man - and he said: He used to recite in Maghrib the short Mufassal."
"1203","1280 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Miktal informed us, from al-Dahhak, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. So this Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) informed from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to recite in the Maghrib prayer the short Mufassal. If we carry the hadith of Jubayr and the reports we reported with it as our opponent carried it, those reports contradict this hadith of Abu Hurayrah; and if we carry them as we mentioned, they agree with this hadith. And it is more fitting for us to carry the reports upon agreement, not contradiction. So it is established by what we mentioned that what should be recited in the Maghrib prayer is the short Mufassal, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad - may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And the like of that was reported from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1204","1281 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, he said: Abu Musa made me read the letter of Umar to him: Recite in Maghrib the end of the Mufassal."
"1205","18 - Chapter of recitation behind the imam. 1282 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Makhul, from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Fajr prayer and the recitation became difficult for him. When he finished he said: Do you recite behind me? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do not do so except with the Opening of the Book, for there is no prayer for whoever does not recite it."
"1206","1283 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from his father Abbad, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Every prayer in which the Mother of the Qur'an is not recited is defective."
"1207","1284 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1208","1285 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Abu al-Sa'ib, the freedman of Hisham ibn Zuhrah, say: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays a prayer and does not recite therein the Mother of the Qur'an, it is defective, incomplete. I said: O Abu Hurayrah, I am sometimes behind the imam. He said: Recite it, O Persian, to yourself."
"1209","1286 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1210","1287 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan informed us, he said: al-Ala narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to these reports and obligated by them recitation behind the imam in all prayers with the Opening of the Book. Others opposed them in that and said: We do not hold that one recites behind the imam in any of the prayers, neither the Opening of the Book nor other than it. And among the proof for them against them is that the two hadiths of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) and A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) which they reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Every prayer in which the Mother of the Qur'an is not recited is defective - there is in that no proof that he meant thereby the prayer behind the imam. It may mean the prayer in which the worshipper has no imam, and he excluded the follower by his saying: Whoever has an imam, the recitation of the imam is recitation for him. So he placed the follower in the ruling of one who recites by his imam's recitation, and the follower is thereby excluded from his saying: Everyone who prays a prayer and does not recite therein the Opening of the Book, his prayer is defective. And we have seen Abu al-Darda heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that the like of this, yet it was not in his view upon the follower."
"1211","1288 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me. (H). 1289 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Darda, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, is there Qur'an in every prayer? He said: Yes. An Ansari man said: It has become obligatory. He said: And Abu al-Darda said: I hold that when the imam leads the people he suffices them. So this Abu al-Darda heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): In every prayer there is Qur'an. A man from the Ansar said: It has become obligatory, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not reject that from the Ansari's saying. Then Abu al-Darda afterwards said from his opinion what he said, and that was in his view upon one who prays alone and upon the imam, not upon the followers. So that opposed the opinion of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that that is upon the follower with the imam, and thereby there remains no proof in that for either group against the other. As for the hadith of Ubadah, it clarified the matter and informed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he commanded the followers to recite behind him the Opening of the Book. So we wanted to see whether anything else contradicted that or not."
"1212","1290 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Ukaymah al-Laythi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished a prayer in which he recited aloud, and said: Did any of you recite with me just now? A man said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was saying, why is the Qur'an being contended with me? He said: So the people stopped reciting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited aloud of the prayers, when they heard that from him."
"1213","1291 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he said: So the Muslims took heed thereby and would not recite."
"1214","1292 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Awwal al-Ahwal narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid Sulayman ibn Hayyan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam was appointed only to be followed, so when he recites, be silent."
"1215","1293 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: They used to recite behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: You have confused my recitation."
"1216","1294 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth informed me, from Ya'qub, from al-Nu'man, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has an imam, the recitation of the imam is recitation for him."
"1217","1295 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, and he did not mention Jabir."
"1218","1296 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from a man of Basra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1219","1297 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur al-Saluli narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Jabir and Layth, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1220","1298 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Jabir - meaning al-Ju'fi - from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1221","1299 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Hayy narrated to us, from Jabir, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), the like of it."
"1222","1300 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever prays a rak'ah and does not recite therein the Mother of the Qur'an has not prayed, except behind the imam."
"1223","1301 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from Jabir, the like of it, and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1224","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Musa ibn ibnat al-Suddi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. He said: I said to Malik: Raise it (to the Prophet). He said: Seize his leg."
"1225","1302 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, then turned with his face and said: Do you recite while the imam is reciting? They were silent, so he asked them three times. They said: We do so. He said: Then do not do so. Abu Ja'far said: We have shown by what we mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts what Ubadah reported. When these reported reports on that differed, we sought its ruling by reasoning. We saw them all agree that a man who comes to the imam while he is bowing says the takbir and bows with him, and that rak'ah counts for him even though he recited nothing therein. That may be because necessity allowed it, fearing to miss the rak'ah, or because recitation behind the imam is not obligatory upon him. We examined that and saw they agree that whoever comes to the imam while he is bowing and bows before entering prayer with a takbir of his own, that does not suffice him, even though he omitted it due to necessity, fearing to miss the rak'ah. So there must be a standing in necessity and fear of missing the rak'ah, just as in non-necessity. These are the descriptions of obligations that are indispensable in prayer, without which prayer does not suffice. When recitation differed from that, falling in necessity, it was of a different kind. So in reasoning it falls in non-necessity as well. This is the reasoning here, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. If one says: It was reported from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they used to recite behind the imam and commanded that. Then he mentioned what:"
"1226","1303 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani informed us, from Jawwab ibn Ubaydullah al-Taymi, he said: Yazid ibn Sharik, Abu Ibrahim al-Taymi, narrated to us that he said: I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) about recitation behind the imam, and he said to me: Recite. I said: Even if I am behind you? He said: Even if you are behind me. I said: Even if you recite? He said: Even if I recite."
"1227","1304 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr informed us, from Mujahid, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr reciting behind the imam in the Zuhr prayer from Surah Maryam."
"1228","1305 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Husayn, he said: I heard Mujahid say: I prayed with Abdullah ibn Amr Zuhr and Asr, and he used to recite behind the imam. It is said to him: This was reported from those you mentioned, and the opposite was reported from others."
"1229","1306 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla - and he passed by the house of Ibn al-Asbahani - he said: The owner of this house narrated to me, and he had recited to Abu Abd al-Rahman, from al-Mukhtar ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Layla, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Whoever recites behind the imam is not upon the fitrah."
"1230","1307 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Listen attentively to the recitation, for in prayer there is preoccupation, and the imam will suffice you that."
"1231","1308 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir - or Abu Jabir, Abu Ja'far said: I doubt - narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"1232","1309 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, the like of it."
"1233","1310 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Would that the one who recites behind the imam had his mouth filled with dust."
"1234","1311 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zubayr, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, the like of it."
"1235","1312 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh informed me, from Bakr ibn Amr, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, that he asked Abdullah ibn Umar, Zayd ibn Thabit and Jabir ibn Abdullah, and they said: Do not recite behind the imam in any of the prayers."
"1236","1313 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Makhramah informed me, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah, then he mentioned the hadith the like of that."
"1237","1314 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he heard him say: Do not recite behind the imam in any of the prayers."
"1238","1315 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Qusayt, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd, the like of it."
"1239","1316 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas: Shall I recite while the imam is before me? He said: No."
"1240","1317 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', that Abdullah ibn Umar, when asked: Does anyone recite behind the imam? Would say: When one of you prays behind the imam, the recitation of the imam suffices him. And Abdullah ibn Umar would not recite behind the imam."
"1241","1318 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: The recitation of the imam suffices you. So these are a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who agreed upon leaving recitation behind the imam. And what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we mentioned earlier agrees with them, and reasoning supports them by what we mentioned, so that is worthier than what opposes it."
"1242","19 - Chapter: lowering in prayer, is there takbir in it? 1319 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-Hasan ibn Imran, from Ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, that he prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would not complete the takbir."
"1243","1320 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this, so they would not say takbir in prayer when lowering, but would say takbir when rising, and thus the Umayyads used to do. Others opposed them in that and said takbir in lowering and rising together, and they went in that to what was successively reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1244","1321 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father and Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying takbir in every lowering and rising."
"1245","1322 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' narrated to us, from Zuhayr, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. He said: And I saw Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) doing that."
"1246","1323 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, he said: Salim al-Barrad narrated to me - and he was in my view more trustworthy than myself - he said: Abu Mas'ud al-Badri said to us: Shall I not pray for you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So he prayed four rak'ahs with us, saying takbir therein each time he lowered and rose, and said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1247","1324 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, he said: Abdullah al-Danaj narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) led us in prayer, and he would say takbir when rising and when lowering. I came to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him of that, and he said: Is that not the Sunnah of Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"1248","1325 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr informed us, from Ikrimah, the like of it, and he did not mention Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1249","1326 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, he said: Abu Musa al-Ash'ari said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) reminded us of a prayer we used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - either we forgot it or we deliberately left it - saying takbir each time he lowered, each time he rose, and each time he prostrated."
"1250","1327 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us. (H). 1328 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the imam says takbir and prostrates, say takbir and prostrate."
"1251","1329 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Sufyan, he said: Abd al-Rahman al-Asamm narrated to me, he said: I heard Anas say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) would complete the takbir; they would say takbir when they prostrated, when they rose, and when they stood from the rak'ah."
"1252","1330 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Rahman al-Asamm, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1253","1331 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, that Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to lead them in the obligatory prayer, saying takbir each time he lowered and rose. When he finished he said: By Allah, I am the most resembling of you in prayer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1254","1332 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man narrating from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah and Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to lead them in the obligatory prayer, then he mentioned the like of it."
"1255","1333 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), the like of it."
"1256","1334 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Sam'an, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say takbir each time he prostrated and rose."
"1257","1335 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya narrated to me that Abu Salamah said: I saw Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying takbir in prayer each time he lowered and rose. I said: O Abu Hurayrah, what is this prayer? He said: It is indeed the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about takbir in every lowering and rising are more manifest than the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, and more successive. And Abu Bakr, Umar and Ali (may Allah be pleased with them) acted upon them after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and practice with them continued to our day, which no denier denies and no rejecter rejects. Then reasoning also supports it: we saw entry into prayer is by takbir, then coming out of bowing and prostration are also by takbir, and likewise standing from sitting is also by takbir. So the changes of states from one state to another are agreed to contain takbir. So by analogy, changing states from standing to bowing and to prostration also contains takbir, by analogy with what we mentioned. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad - may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"1258","20 - Chapter of takbir for bowing and takbir for prostration and rising from bowing: is there raising (of hands) with that or not? 1336 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when he stood for the obligatory prayer he would say takbir and raise his hands level with his shoulders, and do the like when he finished his recitation and wanted to bow, and do it when he finished and rose from bowing, and would not raise his hands in anything of his prayer while sitting, and when he stood from the two prostrations he would raise his hands likewise and say takbir."
"1259","1337 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he opened the prayer raising his hands until level with his shoulders, and when he wanted to bow and after he rose, and he would not raise between the two prostrations."
"1260","1338 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he opened the prayer, would raise his hands level with his shoulders, and when he said takbir for bowing, and when he rose from bowing, he would raise them likewise, and say: Allah hears whoever praises Him; our Lord, to You is praise. And he would not do that between the two prostrations."
"1261","1339 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1262","1340 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd, from Jabir, he said: I saw Salim ibn Abdullah raising his hands level with his shoulders in prayer three times: when opening the prayer, when bowing, and when raising his head. Jabir said: I asked Salim about that, and Salim said: I saw Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) doing that. And Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing that."
"1263","1341 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi among ten of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them Abu Qatadah. He said: Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: Why? By Allah, you were neither the most following him among us nor the earliest in his companionship. He said: Yes indeed. They said: Then show us. He said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer, he would raise his hands until level with his shoulders, then say takbir, then recite, then say takbir and raise his hands until level with his shoulders, then bow, then raise his head and say: Allah hears whoever praises Him, then raise his hands until level with his shoulders, then say: Allah is Greatest, going down to the ground. When he stood from the two rak'ahs he would say takbir and raise his hands until level with his shoulders, then do the like in the rest of his prayer. He said: They all said: You have spoken the truth; thus he used to pray."
"1264","1342 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abbas ibn Sahl, he said: Abu Humayd, Abu Usayd and Sahl ibn Sa'd gathered and mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood he would raise his hands, then raise his hands when saying takbir for bowing, and when he raised his head from bowing he would raise his hands."
"1265","1343 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wa'il ibn Hujr, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when saying takbir for prayer and when raising his head from bowing, raising his hands level with his ears."
"1266","1344 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Asim, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1267","1345 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Nasr ibn Asim, from Malik ibn al-Huwayrith, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he bowed and when he raised his head from his bowing, raising his hands until level above his ears."
"1268","1346 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise his hands when opening the prayer, when bowing, and when prostrating. Abu Ja'far said: A group went to these reports and obligated raising at bowing, at rising from bowing, and at standing to the standing position from sitting in all prayer. Others opposed them in that and said: We do not hold raising except in the first takbir."
"1269","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara ibn Azib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir to open the prayer, he would raise his hands until his thumbs were near the lobes of his ears, then he would not repeat. And they argued in that by what:"
"1270","1347 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from al-Bara ibn Azib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1271","1348 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his brother, and from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1272","1349 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he would raise his hands in the first takbir, then not repeat."
"1273","1350 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"1274","1351 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, he said: I said to Ibrahim: The hadith of Wa'il that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising his hands when opening the prayer, when bowing, and when raising his head from bowing? He said: If Wa'il saw him once doing that, Abdullah saw him fifty times not doing that."
"1275","1352 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I entered the mosque of Hadramawt, and Alqamah ibn Wa'il was narrating from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise his hands before bowing and after it. I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he became angry and said: He saw him, but Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) and his companions did not see him. This was among what the people of this view argued for their view from what we reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And among the proof of their opponent against them was that he said: What we reported is by succession of reports, soundness of chains and uprightness, so our view is worthier than yours. And among the proof against them in that is what we shall explain, Allah the Exalted willing. As for what was reported concerning that from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the hadith of Ibn Abi al-Zinad with which we began mentioning in the first of this chapter."
"1276","1353 - For Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, from his father, that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) would raise his hands in the first takbir of prayer, then not raise after."
"1277","1354 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated to us, from Asim, from his father - and he was among the companions of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) - from Ali, the like of it. So this hadith of Asim ibn Kulayb indicates that the hadith of Ibn Abi al-Zinad is upon one of two aspects: either it is unsound in itself, or there is no mention of raising in it at all, as others reported it."
"1278","1355 - For Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us. (H). 1356 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih and al-Wahbi narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah informed us, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl. Then they mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Abi al-Zinad in its chain and text, and did not mention raising in any of that. If this is the preserved one and the hadith of Ibn Abi al-Zinad is a mistake, then no proof remains for you by a mistaken hadith. And if what Ibn Abi al-Zinad reported is sound because he added to what others reported, then Ali would not have seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising, then abandon raising after him, unless the abrogation of raising was established with him. So the hadith of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), if sound, contains the strongest proof for the view of whoever does not hold raising. As for the hadith of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), there was reported from him what we mentioned from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then there was reported from him of his practice after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the opposite of that."
"1279","1357 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Husayn, from Mujahid, he said: I prayed behind Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), and he would not raise his hands except in the first takbir of prayer. So this Ibn Umar saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising, then he abandoned raising after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). That can only be because the abrogation of what he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do was established with him, and proof was established upon him thereby. If one says: This is a rejected hadith, it is said to him: What indicates that? You will find no way to that. If he says: Tawus mentioned that he saw Ibn Umar doing what agrees with what was reported from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of that. It is said to them: Tawus mentioned that, but Mujahid opposed him. So it is possible that Ibn Umar did what Tawus saw him doing before proof of its abrogation was established with him, then proof of its abrogation was established with him, so he abandoned it and did what Mujahid mentioned from him. Thus what was reported from them should be carried, and error negated from them, until that is verified; otherwise most narrations fall. As for the hadith of Wa'il, Ibrahim opposed it by what he mentioned from Abdullah, that he had not seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do what he mentioned. And Abdullah is earlier in companionship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and more understanding of his actions than Wa'il. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to love that the Emigrants stand near him to preserve from him."
"1280","1358 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to love that the Emigrants and Ansar stand near him, to preserve from him. And as:"
"1281","1359 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let those of understanding and wisdom stand near me. As:"
"1282","1360 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman informed me, he said: I heard Umarah ibn Umayr narrating from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Let those of understanding and wisdom stand near me, then those next to them, then those next to them. And as:"
"1283","1361 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, from Iyas ibn Qatadah, from Qays ibn Ubad, he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Be in the row next to me. Abu Ja'far said: So Abdullah is among those who used to draw near the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to learn how his actions in prayer were, to teach the people that. So what they related of that is worthier than what was brought by whoever was farther from him than them in prayer. If they say: What you mentioned from Ibrahim, from Abdullah, is disconnected. It is said to them: When Ibrahim sent (a mursal report) from Abdullah, he would not send it except after its soundness with him and succession of narration from Abdullah. Al-A'mash said to him: When you narrate to me, connect the chain. He said: When I say to you: Abdullah said, I do not say that until a group narrated it to me from Abdullah; and when I say: So-and-so narrated to me from Abdullah, then it is the one who narrated to me."
"1284","1362 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us with that, he said: Wahb - or Bishr ibn Umar, Abu Ja'far doubted - narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-A'mash, with that. Abu Ja'far said: So he informed that what he sent from Abdullah, its source with him is sounder than the source of what he mentioned from a specific man from Abdullah. Likewise this which he sent from Abdullah, he sent only while its source with him is sounder than the source of what he reports from a specific man from Abdullah. And besides that, we reported it connected in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, and thus Abdullah used to do in the rest of his prayer. As:"
"1285","1363 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah would not raise his hands in anything of prayer except in the opening. And the like of that was also reported from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), as:"
"1286","1364 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ayyash, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abjar, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) raising his hands in the first takbir, then not repeating. He said: And I saw Ibrahim and al-Sha'bi doing that. Abu Ja'far said: So this Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would not raise his hands either except in the first takbir in this hadith, and it is a sound hadith, for al-Hasan ibn Ayyash - even though this hadith revolves upon him - is trustworthy, a proof; Yahya ibn Ma'in and others mentioned that. Do you think it was hidden from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to raise his hands in bowing and prostration, and one below him knew of that, or one with him saw him doing other than what he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing, then none rejected that upon him? With us this is impossible. And the practice of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving him upon that is a sound proof that that is the truth which no one should oppose. As for what they reported from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) of that, it is only from the hadith of Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Salih ibn Kaysan. And they do not make Isma'il a proof in what is reported from other than the Syrians, so how do they argue against their opponent by what, had he argued the like against them, they would not allow him. As for the hadith of Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), they claim it is a mistake, and that none raised it except Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi alone, while the memorizers stop it upon Anas (may Allah be pleased with him). As for the hadith of Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, they weaken Abd al-Hamid, so they establish no proof by him; so how do they argue by him in the like of this. And besides that, Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata did not hear that hadith from Abu Humayd nor from those mentioned with him in that hadith; between them is an unknown man. Al-Attaf ibn Khalid mentioned that from him, from a man, and I shall mention that in the chapter of sitting in prayer, Allah the Exalted willing. And the hadith of Abu Asim from Abd al-Hamid, therein is: They all said: You have spoken the truth - and none says that other than Abu Asim."
"1287","1365 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us. (H). 1366 - And Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, then they mentioned it with its chain, and they did not say: They all said: You have spoken the truth. And thus others besides Abd al-Hamid reported it. And we mentioned it in the chapter of sitting in prayer. We do not see uncovering these reports obliging whoever stands upon their realities and uncovers their sources except abandoning raising in bowing. This is the aspect of this chapter by reports. Abu Ja'far said: I did not intend by anything of that weakening anyone of the people of knowledge, nor is that my way; rather I intended showing the injustice of the opponent to us. As for the aspect of this chapter by reasoning: they agreed the first takbir is accompanied by raising, and the takbir between the two prostrations has no raising with it. And they differed about the takbir of standing and the takbir of bowing. A group said: Their ruling is the ruling of the opening takbir, and in them is raising as in it. Others said: Their ruling is the ruling of the takbir between the two prostrations, and there is no raising in them as there is none in it. And we saw the opening takbir is from the essence of prayer; prayer does not suffice without it. And we saw the takbir between the two prostrations is not so, for had one omitted it, his prayer would not be invalid. And we saw the takbir of bowing and the takbir of standing are not from the essence of prayer, for had one omitted them his prayer would not be invalid, and they are from its Sunnahs. When they are from the Sunnah of prayer, as the takbir between the two prostrations is from the Sunnah of prayer, they are like it in that there is no raising in them as there is none in it. This is the reasoning in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And:"
"1288","1367 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to me, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: I never saw a jurist at all doing it - raising his hands in other than the first takbir."
"1289","21 - Chapter of clasping (tatbiq) in bowing. 1368 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isra'il informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, that they entered upon Abdullah and he said: Did these pray behind you? They said: Yes. He stood between them and placed one of them on his right and the other on his left. Then we bowed and placed our hands on our knees, so he struck our hands and clasped, then clasped with his hands and placed them between his thighs. When he prayed he said: Thus the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did."
"1290","1369 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, that they were with Abdullah, then he mentioned the like of it."
"1291","1370 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim narrated to me, from al-Aswad, he said: I and Alqamah entered upon Abdullah, and he said: Did these pray behind you? We said: Yes. He said: Then pray. He led us and did not command us with adhan nor iqamah. We stood behind him, so he brought us forward, and one of us stood on his right and the other on his left. When he bowed he placed his hands between his legs and bent. He said: And he struck my hands upon my knees and said: Thus - and he gestured with his hand. When he prayed he said: When you are three, pray together; and when you are more than that, put one of you forward. When one of you bows, let him say thus - and he clasped his hands - then spread his forearms between his thighs; it is as if I am looking at the fingers of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ja'far said: A group went to this and argued by this hadith. Others opposed them in that and said: Rather he should, when he bows, place his hands on his knees as one grasping them, and separate his fingers. And they argued in that by what:"
"1292","1371 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar and Hayyan ibn Hilal narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hisn informed me, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, he said: Umar said: Touch - for the knees were made Sunnah for you."
"1293","1372 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, he said: Salim al-Barrad narrated to us - and he was with me more trustworthy than myself - he said: Abu Mas'ud al-Badri said to us: Shall I not show you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Then he mentioned a long hadith. He said: Then he bowed and placed his palms on his knees, and his fingers were separated upon his shins."
"1294","1373 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abbas ibn Sahl, he said: Abu Humayd, Abu Usayd, Sahl ibn Sa'd and Muhammad ibn Maslamah gathered - as Ibn Marzuq supposes - and mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he bowed he would place his hands on his knees as one grasping them."
"1295","1374 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi among ten of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them Abu Qatadah, then he mentioned the like of it. He said: They all said: You have spoken the truth."
"1296","1375 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wa'il ibn Hujr, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he bowed placing his hands on his knees."
"1297","1376 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, he said: I heard Ibn Ajlan narrating from one named, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: The people complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about spreading in prayer, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek help by the knees. So these reports opposed the first report, and with them is succession which is not with it. We wanted to see whether in any of these reports is what indicates abrogation of one of the two matters by the other. So we examined that."
"1298","1377 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ya'fur, he said: I heard Mus'ab ibn Sa'd say: I prayed beside my father and placed my hands between my knees, so he struck my hands and said: O my son, we used to do this, then we were commanded to strike with the palms upon the knees."
"1299","1378 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Ya'fur, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1300","1379 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, he said: I prayed with Sa'd, and when I wanted to bow I clasped, so he forbade me from it and said: We used to do it until we were forbidden from it. So it is established by what we mentioned that clasping is abrogated, and that it preceded what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did of placing the hands upon the knees. Then we sought its ruling by reasoning, how is it? We saw clasping contains joining of the hands, and we saw placing the hands upon the knees contains separating them. We wanted to see the ruling of the like forms in prayer, how is it. We saw the Sunnah came from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with keeping apart in bowing and prostration, and the Muslims agreed upon that, and that is from separating the limbs. And whoever stands in prayer is commanded to alternate between his feet, and that was reported from Ibn Mas'ud, and he is the one who reported clasping. When we saw separating the limbs therein, some from others, is worthier than joining some to others, and they differed about joining them and separating them in bowing, the analogy is that what they differed about thereof is attached to what they agreed upon thereof, so as separating in what we mentioned is better, it is so in the rest of the limbs. And there was reported about keeping apart in prostration what:"
"1301","1380 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Tamimi, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits would be seen."
"1302","1381 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Hisham and Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, they said: Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn al-Asamm narrated to me, from Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated, he would keep apart until whoever was behind him saw the brightness of his armpits."
"1303","1382 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sayyah narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Burqan and Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Asamm, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Maymunah, the like of it."
"1304","1383 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prostrated would keep apart until the whiteness of his armpits was seen - or: until I saw the whiteness of his armpits."
"1305","1384 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Mughirah, he said: Abu al-Haytham narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Sa'id say: It is as if I am looking at the whiteness of the sides of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he is prostrating."
"1306","1385 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I saw al-Bara' when he prostrated spread his arms out and raised his buttocks, and he said: This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"1307","1386 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, from Abdullah ibn Buhaynah, that he narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrated would spread his arms apart from his sides until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."
"1308","1387 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi' informed me, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Aqram al-Ka'bi, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, and I looked at the whiteness of his armpits, that is the white of his armpits, while he was prostrating."
"1309","1388 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nafi' ibn Yazid informed me, he said: Khalid ibn Yazid informed me, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Mughirah, from Abu al-Haytham, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: It is as if I am looking at the whiteness of the sides of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was prostrating."
"1310","1389 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym and Affan narrated to us, they said: Abbad ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Ahmar, the Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to me, he said: We would feel pity for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) because of how he kept his hands away from his sides when he prostrated."
"1311","1390 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Abu Amir narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Maysarah, from al-Hasan, he said: Ahmar, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed me of the same. Since the Sunnah in what we have mentioned is to separate the limbs, not to press them together, so it is in what we have mentioned likewise. Thus by the establishment of the abrogation which we mentioned, and by the abrogation which we described, the removal of the practice of joining the hands between the knees is established, and the obligation of placing the hands on the knees. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"1312","Chapter 22: The measure of bowing and prostration below which it does not suffice. 1391 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Yazid, from Awn ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: When one of you says in his bowing, Glory be to my Lord the Most Great, three times, his bowing is complete, and that is its minimum; and when he says in his prostration, Glory be to my Lord the Most High, three times, his prostration is complete, and that is its minimum."
"1313","1392 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: A group of people adopted this view and said: the measure of bowing and prostration below which it does not suffice is this, and they argued for that with this hadith. Others differed with them and said: the measure of bowing is that one bows until he is settled while bowing, and the measure of prostration is that one prostrates until he is still while prostrating; this is the measure of bowing and prostration which is indispensable."
"1314","And they argued for that with what: 1393 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abi Namir narrated to me, from Ali ibn Yahya, from his uncle Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting in the mosque when a man entered and prayed, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was looking at him, and he said to him: When you stand in your prayer, say the takbir, then recite if you have Qur'an with you; if you do not have Qur'an with you, then praise Allah, say the takbir and say la ilaha illa Allah, then bow until you are still while bowing, then stand until you stand upright, then prostrate until you are still while prostrating, then sit until you are still while sitting; if you do that your prayer is complete, and whatever you fall short of that, you only fall short of your prayer."
"1315","1394 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad al-Zuraqi, from his father, from his grandfather Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"1316","1395 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in these two hadiths of the obligation which is indispensable and without which the prayer is not complete. So we know that whatever is besides that is only intended as the least by which excellence is sought, even if the hadith in which that is mentioned is disconnected from him and not equal to these two hadiths in their chains. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"1317","Chapter 23: What should be said in bowing and prostration. 1396 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say while bowing: O Allah, to You I have bowed, in You I have believed, to You I have submitted, and You are my Lord; my hearing, my sight, my brain, my bones and my sinews have humbled themselves to Allah, Lord of the worlds; and he would say in his prostration: O Allah, to You I have prostrated, to You I have submitted, and You are my Lord; my face has prostrated to the One who created it and gave it hearing and sight; blessed is Allah, the best of creators."
"1318","1397 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us. (H) 1398 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi and Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Majishun informed us, from al-Majishun and Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from al-A'raj, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1319","1399 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he bowed would say: O Allah, to You I have bowed, in You I have believed, to You I have submitted, You are my Lord; my hearing, my sight, my brain, my bones and what my feet carry have humbled themselves to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"1320","1400 - Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Nu'man ibn Sa'd, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been forbidden to recite while bowing or prostrating. As for bowing, glorify the Lord in it; as for prostration, strive in supplication, for it is likely to be answered for you."
"1321","1401 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Uyaynah say: Sulayman ibn Suhaym narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from his father, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted the curtain while the people were in rows behind Abu Bakr, then he mentioned the same."
"1322","1402 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say often in his bowing: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You; so forgive me, for You are the Most Relenting."
"1323","1403 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us. (H) 1404 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, and they mentioned the same with its chain."
"1324","1405 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Kunasi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1325","1406 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his bowing and prostration: All-Glorious, All-Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit."
"1326","1407 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1327","1408 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Faraj ibn Fudalah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I missed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night, and I thought he had gone to his slave girl, so I searched for him with my hand and my hand fell on the soles of his feet while he was prostrating, saying: O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your anger, and I seek refuge in Your pardon from Your punishment, and I seek refuge in You from You; I cannot count praise of You, You are as You have praised Yourself."
"1328","1409 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, that A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said, then he mentioned the same."
"1329","1410 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu al-Nadr say: I heard Urwah say: A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said, and he mentioned the same, except that he did not mention his saying, I cannot count praise of You, and he added: I praise You, I cannot encompass all that is in You."
"1330","1411 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Sumayy the freed slave of Abu Bakr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his prostration: O Allah, forgive me all my sins, the small of them and the great, the first of them and the last, the open of them and the secret."
"1331","1412 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Sumayy the freed slave of Abu Bakr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The closest the servant is to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is while he is prostrating, so increase supplication. Abu Ja'far said: A group of people adopted these reports to hold that there is no harm in a man supplicating in his bowing and prostration with whatever he wishes, and that there is nothing fixed in that according to them, and they argued for that with these reports. Others differed with them and said: he should not add in his bowing beyond Glory be to my Lord the Most Great, repeating it as much as he wishes, and he should not fall short of three times in that; and he should not add in his prostration beyond Glory be to my Lord the Most High, repeating it as much as he wishes, and he should not fall short of that by less than three times. And they argued for that with what follows."
"1332","1413 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from his uncle Iyas ibn Amir al-Ghafiqi, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, he said: When So glorify the name of your Lord, the Most Great was revealed, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Put it in your bowing; and when Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High was revealed, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Put it in your prostration."
"1333","1414 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayyub narrated to me, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1334","1415 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Amir, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned the same. And among their argument for that was that what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the first reports may have been before the revelation of the two verses we mentioned in the hadith of Uqbah. Then when they were revealed, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them with what he commanded them of that, so his command abrogated what had preceded of his practice. And it has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to say in his bowing and prostration what he was commanded in the hadith of Uqbah."
"1335","1416 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Sa'd ibn Ubaydah, from al-Mustawrid, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfah, that he prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night, and he would say in his bowing, Glory be to my Lord the Most Great, and in his prostration, Glory be to my Lord the Most High."
"1336","('1336', ""1417 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Suhaym al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Silah, from Hudhayfah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his bowing, Glory be to my Lord the Most Great, three times, and in his prostration, Glory be to my Lord the Most High, three times. This too has indicated what we mentioned of his keeping to a specific supplication in bowing and prostration. Others said: as for bowing, nothing is added in it beyond glorifying the Lord, Mighty and Majestic; as for prostration, one strives in it in supplication. And they argued for that with the two hadiths of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) which we mentioned in the first section. Among the argument against them in that is that they made the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as for bowing glorify the Lord in it, an abrogator of what had preceded of his practices before that in the first hadiths. So it is possible that his commanding them with glorification in bowing was before there was revealed to him So glorify the name of your Lord, the Most Great, and his urging them to supplication in prostration with whatever they wished was before there was revealed to him Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High; then when that was revealed to him, he commanded them to keep to it in their prostrations according to what is in the hadith of Uqbah, and not to add upon it, so that became an abrogator of what had preceded of it before that, just as what he commanded them in bowing upon the revelation of So glorify the name of your Lord, the Most Great was an abrogator of what had been from him before that. If one says: that was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) near his death, because in the hadith of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted the curtain while the people were in rows behind Abu Bakr, it is said to him: is there in this hadith that that prayer after which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, or that that illness was the illness in which he died? There is nothing of this in the hadith. And it is possible that it was the prayer after which he died, and possible that it was another prayer after which he recovered. If that was the prayer after which he died, it is possible that Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High was revealed to him after that before his death. And if that prayer was earlier than that, it is even more possible that after it there was what we mentioned. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of establishing the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning, we have seen places in the prayer in which there is remembrance. Of that is the takbir for entering the prayer, and of that is the takbir for bowing, prostrating and standing from sitting. That takbir was a takbir which the servants were kept to and taught, and they were not allowed to go beyond it to other than it. And of that is what they testify with in sitting, for they were taught it and kept to it, and they were not allowed to bring in its place a remembrance other than it, because if a man said in place of his saying Allah is Most Great, Allah is Greatest or Allah is Most Majestic, he would be doing wrong. And if a man testified with wording differing from the wording of the tashahhud which the reports brought from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions, he would be doing wrong; and after finishing the last tashahhud he has been permitted supplication of whatever he loves, so it was said to him in what Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): then let him choose of supplication what he loves. So in every remembrance he was kept to a specific remembrance, and going beyond it to what he loves was not allowed except what he was kept to of that, even if that is equal in meaning. Since in bowing and prostration it is agreed that in them there is remembrance, and it is not agreed that he is permitted in them every remembrance, reasoning upon that is that that remembrance be like the rest of the remembrance in his prayer, of his takbir and his tashahhud, and his saying Allah hears whoever praises Him and the saying of the follower Our Lord, to You is praise, so it is a specific saying which no one should go beyond to other than it, just as he should not in the rest of the remembrance which is in the prayer, and he may not go beyond that to other than it except by instruction from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon that. By that the saying of those who fixed a specific remembrance in that is established, and they are those who adopted the hadith of Uqbah, according to what is detailed in it of the saying in bowing and prostration. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. If one says: where was it allowed for the worshipper to say after the tashahhud what he loves? It is said to him: in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud:"")"
"1337","1418 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us with that, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, he said: We used to say behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when we sat in prayer: Peace be upon Allah and upon His servants, peace be upon Jibril and Mika'il, peace be upon so-and-so and so-and-so. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is al-Salam, so do not say that, but say: and they mentioned the tashahhud as we mentioned it elsewhere than this place, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Then let one of you after that choose the best speech, or what he loves of speech."
"1338","1419 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: We did not know what to say between every two rak'ahs, except that we would glorify Allah, say the takbir and praise our Lord; and Muhammad was given the openings of speech and its comprehensive conciseness, or he said its seals; so he said: When you sit in the two rak'ahs, say: and he mentioned the tashahhud, then let one of you choose of supplication what pleases him most, and supplicate his Lord with it."
"1339","1420 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: Then let him choose of speech afterwards whatever he wishes. Here he was permitted to choose of supplication what he loves, because what is besides it of the prayer is different from it. Of that is what we mentioned of the takbir in its places, and of the tashahhud in its place, and of the opening supplication in its place, and of the salutation in its place; that was made a specific remembrance not extended to other than it. By analogy to that, the remembrance in bowing and prostration is likewise a specific remembrance, not extended to other than it."
"1340","('1340', ""Chapter 24: The imam says, Allah hears whoever praises Him; should he say after it, Our Lord, to You is praise, or not? 1421 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam, Abu Awanah and Aban narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Hittan ibn Abdullah, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us the prayer, and he said: When the imam says the takbir, say the takbir; when he bows, bow; when he prostrates, prostrate; and when he says, Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise; Allah will hear you, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah hears whoever praises Him."")"
"1341","1422 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1342","1423 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn Ata, he said: I heard Abu Alqamah narrating, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it, except that he did not mention his saying: Allah will hear you, to the end of the hadith."
"1343","1424 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1344","1425 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1345","('1345', ""1426 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Sumayy, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the imam says, Allah hears whoever praises Him, then say: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise; for whoever's saying coincides with the saying of the angels, his past sins are forgiven. A group of people adopted the view that these reports have indicated to them what the imam and the follower both say, and that the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he says, Allah hears whoever praises Him, then say: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise, is proof that Allah hears whoever praises Him is said by the imam to the exclusion of the follower, and that Our Lord, to You is praise is said by the follower to the exclusion of the imam. Among those who adopted this saying are Abu Hanifah and Malik, may Allah have mercy on them both. Others differed with them and said: rather the imam says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, our Lord, to You is praise, then the follower says: Our Lord, to You is praise, specifically. And they said: there is no proof in the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the imam says, Allah hears whoever praises Him, then say: Our Lord, to You is praise, that the follower says that to the exclusion of others. Had it been so, it would be impossible for one who is not a follower to say it. You agree unanimously that one praying alone says it along with his saying Allah hears whoever praises Him; just as one who prays alone says it while he is not a follower, and what we mentioned of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not negate that, so the imam also says it likewise, and what we mentioned of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not negate that. And they argued for that with what follows."")"
"1346","1427 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when he raised his head from bowing he would say: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise filling the heaven and filling the earth and filling whatever You will of things besides. And with what follows."
"1347","1428 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1348","1429 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd, that is Ibn Hasan Abu al-Hasan, informed me, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Awfa narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1349","1430 - Malik ibn Abdullah ibn Sayf narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Tanukhi informed us, from Atiyyah ibn Qays al-Kala'i, from Qaza'ah ibn Yahya, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, with the addition: O people of praise and glory, most true of what the servant says, and all of us are servants to You; there is no withholder of what You give, and the fortune of the fortunate does not avail him against You."
"1350","1431 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Sharik, from Abu Amr, that is al-Munabbihi, from Abu Juhayfah, he said: Fortunes were mentioned before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so some of the people said: the fortune of so-and-so is in camels, and some said: in horses; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent. Then when he stood to pray and raised his head from bowing, he said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise filling the heaven and filling the earth and filling whatever You will of things besides; there is no withholder of what You give, no giver of what You withhold, and the fortune of the fortunate does not avail him against You. There is nothing in these reports that he used to say that while he was an imam, nor in them anything indicating any of that. Except that it is established by them that one who prays alone says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, our Lord, to You is praise. So we wanted to examine: has there been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything indicating the ruling of the imam in that, how it is, and whether he says of that what one who prays alone says or not?"
"1351","Yunus narrated to us with that, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that they heard him say: 1432 - The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he finished the recitation of the Fajr prayer and said the takbir and raised his head from bowing would say: Allah hears whoever praises Him, our Lord, to You is praise; O Allah, save al-Walid ibn al-Walid, then he mentioned the hadith. It is also possible that he said that because it was part of the qunut, then abandoned it afterwards when he abandoned the qunut. So we returned to other than this hadith: is there in it any indication of anything of what we mentioned?"
"1352","Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us with that, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: 1433 - I am the most like you in prayer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he said, Allah hears whoever praises Him, he said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise."
"1353","Yunus informed me with that, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: 1434 - The sun eclipsed in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he prayed with the people; then when he raised his head from bowing he said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, our Lord, to You is praise."
"1354","('1354', ""1435 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he stood up from bowing would say that. In these reports is what indicates that the imam says of that the same as one who prays alone says, because in the hadith of A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that while he was praying with the people. And in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), I am the most like you in prayer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned that. So he informed that what he did of that is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do in his prayer, doing nothing else. And in the hadith of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) is what we mentioned from him, and it too informs of the description of his prayer, how it was. When it is established from him that he used to say, while he was an imam, when he raised his head from bowing, Allah hears whoever praises Him, our Lord, to You is praise, it is established that thus the imam should do that, following what is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for by way of reasoning, they have agreed concerning one who prays alone that he says that. So we wanted to examine concerning the imam: is his ruling in that the ruling of one who prays alone or not? We found the imam does in all his prayer, of takbir, recitation, standing, sitting and tashahhud, the same as one who prays alone does. And we found his rulings in what befalls him in his prayer like the rulings of one who prays alone in what befalls him of his prayer, of things which necessitate its corruption, what necessitates the prostration of forgetfulness in it, and other than that; and the imam and one who prays alone are alike in that, unlike the follower. When it is established by their agreement that one praying alone says after his saying Allah hears whoever praises Him, Our Lord, to You is praise, it is established that the imam also says it after his saying Allah hears whoever praises Him. This is the aspect of reasoning also in this chapter; by this we take, and it is the saying of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both. As for Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, he adopted in that the first saying."")"
"1355","Chapter 25: The qunut in the Fajr prayer and others. 1436 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id and Abu Salamah, that they heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say when he finished the recitation of the Fajr prayer and said the takbir and raised his head, saying: Allah hears whoever praises Him, our Lord, to You is praise, saying while standing: O Allah, save al-Walid ibn al-Walid, Salamah ibn Hisham, Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, and the oppressed among the believers; O Allah, make Your grip severe upon Mudar, and make it upon them like the years of Yusuf; O Allah, curse Lihyan, Ri'l, Dhakwan and Usayyah; Usayyah disobeyed Allah and His Messenger."
"1356","1437 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prayed the last Isha and raised his head from bowing would say: O Allah, save al-Walid ibn al-Walid, then he mentioned the same."
"1357","1438 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, he said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I shall show you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a word similar to it. When he raised his head from bowing and said, Allah hears whoever praises Him, he would supplicate for the believers and curse the disbelievers."
"1358","1439 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when he said, Allah hears whoever praises Him, in the last rak'ah of the Isha prayer, he would say: O Allah, save al-Walid, then he mentioned the same as the hadith of Abu Bakrah, from Abu Dawud."
"1359","1440 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), the same. Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: One morning he did not supplicate for them, so I mentioned that, and he said: Do you not see that they have arrived?"
"1360","1441 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he wanted to supplicate for someone or supplicate against someone would perform qunut after bowing; and sometimes he would say, when he said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, our Lord, to You is praise: O Allah, save al-Walid, then he mentioned the same. Except that he did not mention the saying of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), one morning he did not supplicate for them, to the end of the hadith. And he added, he said: he would say it aloud; and he would say in some of his prayers: O Allah, curse so-and-so and so-and-so, living tribes of the Arabs; so Allah the Exalted revealed: You have no part in the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers."
"1361","1442 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Subh prayer, when he raised his head from bowing, say: Our Lord, to You is praise, in the last rak'ah, then say: O Allah, curse so-and-so and so-and-so, against people of the hypocrites; so Allah the Exalted revealed: You have no part in the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers."
"1362","1443 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Abdullah ibn Ka'b, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he raised his head from the last rak'ah would say: O Allah, save. Then he mentioned the same as the hadith of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, with the addition that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: You have no part in the matter. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not supplicate against anyone after that."
"1363","1444 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara' ibn Azib, he narrated to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform qunut in the Subh and Maghrib prayers."
"1364","1445 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan and Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara', that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform qunut in the Subh and Maghrib prayers."
"1365","1446 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Nusayr, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for thirty days."
"1366","1447 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah ibn Harmalah narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Khufaf, from Khufaf ibn Ima', he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed, then raised his head and said: Ghifar, may Allah forgive her; Aslam, may Allah grant her peace; and Usayyah disobeyed Allah and His Messenger; O Allah, curse Banu Lihyan; O Allah, curse Ri'l and Dhakwan; Allah is Most Great. Then he fell prostrating."
"1367","1448 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Kuthayri al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah al-Laythi, from Khalid ibn Abdullah ibn Harmalah al-Mudliji, from al-Harith ibn Khufaf ibn Ima' ibn Rahdah al-Ghifari, from Khufaf ibn Ima', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Except that he did not mention that when he fell prostrating he said, Allah is Most Great; and he added that Khufaf said: So the cursing of the disbelievers was assigned because of that."
"1368","1449 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1369","1450 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, he said: Anas was asked: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform qunut in the Fajr prayer? He said: Yes. It was said to him, or I said to him: before bowing or after it? He said: After bowing, briefly."
"1370","1451 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept performing qunut in the morning prayer until I parted from him; and I prayed with Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and he kept performing qunut in the morning prayer until I parted from him."
"1371","1452 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for a month, supplicating against Usayyah, Dhakwan, Ri'l and Lihyan."
"1372","1453 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only performed qunut after the rak'ah for a month. He said: I said: Then how is the qunut? He said: Before bowing."
"1373","1454 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Asim, he said: I asked Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) about the qunut: before bowing or after bowing? He said: No, rather before bowing. I said: People claim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut after bowing. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only performed qunut for a month, supplicating against people who killed people of his Companions called the reciters."
"1374","1455 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shadh ibn Fayyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: The qunut was in the Fajr and Maghrib prayers."
"1375","1456 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Mijlaz, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for a month, supplicating against Ri'l and Dhakwan."
"1376","1457 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Hanzalah al-Sadusi narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Among the qunut of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: And make their hearts like the hearts of disbelieving women."
"1377","1458 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, he said: I was sitting with Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) when it was said to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only performed qunut for a month. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept performing qunut in the morning prayer until he left this world."
"1378","1459 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Marwan al-Asfar, he said: I asked Anas: Did Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) perform qunut? He said: One better than Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) performed qunut."
"1379","1460 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for twenty days."
"1380","1461 - al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, from Hanzalah al-Sadusi, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Subh prayer saying the takbir until when he finished he said the takbir and bowed, then raised his head and prostrated, then stood in the second and recited until when he finished he said the takbir and bowed, then raised his head and supplicated."
"1381","1462 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Hammam informed us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for thirty mornings against Ri'l, Dhakwan and Usayyah who disobeyed Allah and His Messenger."
"1382","1463 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for a month after bowing, supplicating against a tribe of the Arab tribes, then abandoned it. Abu Ja'far said: A group of people adopted affirming the qunut in the Fajr prayer, then split into two groups: one group of them said: it is after bowing; and a group said: before bowing. And among those who said that of them are Ibn Abi Layla and Malik ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with them both, as follows."
"1383","1464 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: I heard Malik say: What I have adopted for myself particularly is the qunut in the Fajr before bowing. Among the argument of those of them who held that it is after bowing was what we mentioned from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr. And the argument against them for the other group was what we mentioned in the hadith of Sufyan, from Asim, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only performed qunut after bowing for a month, and that the qunut is before bowing. Others differed with them in that and said: We do not hold the qunut in the Fajr prayer at all, neither before bowing nor after it. And among their argument for that was that these reports narrated on the qunut were narrated as we mentioned. One of those who narrated that from him was Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him); we have narrated from him concerning it that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for thirty days. So the qunut of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and knowledge of it was established with him. Then we have found from him what follows."
"1384","1465 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not perform qunut except for a month; he did not perform qunut before it nor after it."
"1385","1466 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamzah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for a month, supplicating against Usayyah and Dhakwan. When he prevailed over them he abandoned the qunut. And Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) would not perform qunut in the morning prayer. Abu Ja'far said: This Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) informs that the qunut of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which was, was only because of those he was supplicating against, and that he had abandoned that, so the qunut became abrogated, and he after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not perform qunut. One of those who narrated that also from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both). Then he informed them that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abrogated that when He revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): You have no part in the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers. So that became with Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) abrogated also, and he would not perform qunut after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And he would disapprove of one who performed qunut, as follows."
"1386","1467 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: I prayed the Subh behind Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and he did not perform qunut. I said: Does pride prevent you? He said: I do not remember it from any of my companions. And as follows."
"1387","1468 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Mu'ammal narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Abu al-Sha'tha', he said: I asked Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) about the qunut, and he said: I have not witnessed, and I have not seen; thus in the hadith of Wahb, and in the hadith of Mu'ammal: nor have I seen anyone do it. And as follows."
"1388","1469 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from al-Ash'ath, from his father, he said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about the qunut, and he said: What is the qunut? He said: When the imam finishes the recitation in the last rak'ah, he stands supplicating. He said: I have not seen anyone do it, and I think you, O company of the people of Iraq, do it. And as follows."
"1389","1470 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Tamim ibn Salamah, he said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about the qunut, and he mentioned the same except that he said: I have neither seen nor known. The purport of what is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) in this chapter is that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he raised his head from the last rak'ah perform qunut until Allah the Exalted revealed: You have no part in the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers, so he abandoned for that the qunut which he used to perform. Abu Mijlaz asked him and said: Does pride prevent you from the qunut? He said: I do not remember it from any of my companions, meaning from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that is they did not do it after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abandoned it. Abu al-Sha'tha' asked him about the qunut, and Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) asked him about that qunut, what it is, and he informed him that the imam when he finishes the recitation in the last rak'ah of the Subh prayer stands supplicating. He said: I have not seen anyone do it; because what he knew of the qunut of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only supplication after bowing, and as for before bowing he did not see it from him nor from other than him, so he disapproved of that because of it. It is established by what we narrated from him that the qunut of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after bowing was abrogated, and the negation of the qunut before bowing originally, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) neither did it nor his caliphs after him. One of those from whom the qunut from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is narrated was Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and he informed in his hadith which we narrated from him that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut with was supplication against those he was supplicating against, and that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abrogated that with His saying: You have no part in the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, the verse; so in that also is the obligation of abandoning the qunut in the Fajr. One of those from whom that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is also narrated was Khufaf ibn Ima', and he mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when he raised his head from bowing he said: Aslam, may Allah grant her peace, and Ghifar, may Allah forgive her, and Usayyah disobeyed Allah and His Messenger; O Allah, curse Banu Lihyan and those mentioned with them. In this hadith he cursed those the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed, and in the two hadiths of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, they informed in their hadiths that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abandoned that when the verse we mentioned was revealed to him. In their hadiths is the abrogation as in the hadith of Khufaf ibn Ima', and they are more entitled than the hadith of Ibn Ima', and in that is the obligation of abandoning the qunut also. One of those from whom that is also narrated was al-Bara', and it is narrated from him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform qunut in the Fajr and Maghrib, and he did not inform of that qunut of his what it is; so it is possible that that qunut be the qunut which Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr and whoever narrated that with them narrated, then that was abrogated with this verse also. And in this hadith he joined between the Maghrib and the Fajr, mentioning that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform qunut in them. In the agreement of our opponent with us that what he used to do in the Maghrib of that is abrogated, which no one after him may do, is proof that what he used to do in the Fajr is likewise. One of those from whom the qunut in the Fajr from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is also narrated was Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him). Amr ibn Ubayd narrated from al-Hasan from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept performing qunut after bowing in the morning prayer until he parted from him. So in this hadith he affirmed the qunut in the morning prayer and that it was not abrogated. And there has been narrated from him by other chains contrary to that: Ayyub narrated from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: Anas was asked: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform qunut in the Subh prayer? He said: Yes. It was said to him: before bowing or after it? He said: After bowing, briefly. And Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah narrated from him that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qunut for thirty mornings, against Ri'l and Dhakwan. And Qatadah narrated from him similar to that. And Humayd narrated from him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only performed qunut for twenty days. All of these have informed from him contrary to what Amr narrated from al-Hasan; and Asim narrated from him the denial of the qunut after bowing originally, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only did that for a month, but the qunut is before bowing; so that also contradicted what Amr ibn Ubayd narrated and differed with him. So it is not allowed for anyone to argue in the hadith of Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) with one of the two aspects of what is narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), because his opponent may argue against him with what is narrated from Anas of what contradicts that. As for his saying: but the qunut is before bowing, he did not mention that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so it is possible that he took that from those after him, or an opinion he held. Others of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held contrary to that, so his saying is not more entitled than the saying of whoever differed with him except by a proof clarifying to us. If one says: Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, he said: I was sitting with Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) when it was said to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only performed qunut for a month. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept performing qunut in the morning prayer until he left this world, it is said to him: it is possible that that qunut be the qunut which Amr narrated from al-Hasan from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him); if it is so, it has been contradicted by what we mentioned. And it is possible that that qunut be the qunut before bowing which Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned in the hadith of Asim. Nothing is established to us from Anas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the qunut before bowing, and the abrogation of the qunut after bowing is established from him. And Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) was one of those from whom the qunut in the Fajr from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is also narrated; that qunut is supplication for a people and supplication against others. And in his hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abandoned that when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: You have no part in the matter, the verse. If one says: how is it allowed that this be so, when Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform qunut in the Subh, then he mentioned what follows."
"1390","1471 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us. (H) 1472 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from al-A'raj, he said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to perform qunut in the Subh prayer. Abu Ja'far said: That indicated that what was abrogated with Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) was only the supplication against those the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against. As for the qunut which was with that, then no. It is said to him: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated from al-Zuhri in the hadith of the qunut which we narrated at the beginning of this chapter what follows."
"1391","1473 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned that hadith in full. Then he said in it: Then it has reached us that he abandoned that when there was revealed to him: You have no part in the matter, the verse. So the mention of the revelation of this verse by which the abrogation was became from the speech of al-Zuhri, not from what he narrated from Sa'id and Abu Salamah from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him). So it is possible that the revelation of this verse Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) did not know of, so he acted upon what he knew of the practice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his qunut until he died, because the proof was not established with him of contrary to that. And Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr knew that the revelation of this verse was an abrogation of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do, so they adhered to that and abandoned by it the earlier abrogated practice. And another proof is that in the hadith of Ibn Ima' is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, when he raised his head from the rak'ah: Ghifar, may Allah forgive her, until he mentioned what he mentioned in his hadith, then said Allah is Most Great and fell prostrating. By that it is established that all of what he used to say is what was abandoned by the revelation of that verse, and what he used to supplicate with besides that of his supplication for the captives who were in Makkah, then abandoned that when they arrived. And Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) also narrated in the hadith of Yahya ibn Kathir which we narrated earlier in this chapter from him, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), mentioning the qunut. And in it Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: One morning he did not supplicate for them, so I mentioned that, and he said: Do you not see that they have arrived to me? In that is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say that qunut in the last Isha as he used to say it in the Subh, and they have agreed that that is abrogated from the last Isha prayer in its entirety, not to another qunut; so the Fajr is likewise in abrogation. When we uncovered the aspects of these reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the qunut, and did not find them indicating its obligation now in the Fajr prayer, we did not command it in it and commanded its abandonment, along with that some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) denied it originally, as follows."
"1392","1474 - Ali ibn Ma'bad, Husayn ibn Nasr and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Harun. He said: Abu Malik al-Ashja'i Sa'd ibn Tariq informed us, he said: I said to my father: O father, you have prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and behind Abu Bakr and behind Umar and behind Uthman and behind Ali (may Allah be pleased with them), here in Kufah, nearly five years; did they perform qunut in the Fajr? He said: O my son, it is innovated. Abu Ja'far said: We do not say that it is innovated in the sense that it never was, but it was, after which there was what we narrated in what we narrated in this chapter before it. When the qunut from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not established to us, we returned to what is narrated from his Companions in that."
"1393","1475 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us with that, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla informed us, from Ata, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: I prayed the morning prayer behind Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and he performed qunut in it after bowing, and said in his qunut: O Allah, we seek Your help and seek Your forgiveness, and praise You with all good, and thank You and do not disbelieve in You, and cast off and abandon whoever disobeys You; O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry, we hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; Your punishment befalls the disbelievers."
"1394","1476 - And Salih narrated to us with that, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us, from Dharr ibn Abdullah al-Hamdani, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza al-Khuza'i, from his father, that he prayed behind Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and did the same, except that he said: we praise You and do not disbelieve in You, and we fear Your severe punishment."
"1395","1477 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us with that, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) performed qunut in the morning prayer before bowing with the two surahs."
"1396","1478 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to perform qunut in the Subh prayer with two surahs: O Allah, we seek Your help, and O Allah, You alone we worship."
"1397","1479 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Rafi', he said: I prayed the Subh prayer behind Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and he recited al-Ahzab, and I heard his qunut while I was in the last rows."
"1398","1480 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. (H) 1481 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, both from Mukhariq, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: I prayed the Subh prayer behind Umar (may Allah be pleased with him); when he finished the recitation in the second rak'ah he said the takbir then performed qunut, then said the takbir and bowed."
"1399","1482 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mukhariq, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1400","1483 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib was told the saying of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) on the qunut, and he said: He did perform qunut with his father, but he forgot. Abu Ja'far said: There has been narrated from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) what we mentioned, and contrary to that has been narrated from him."
"1401","1484 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us with that, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would not perform qunut in the Subh prayer."
"1402","1485 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad and Amr ibn Maymun, they said: We prayed the Fajr behind Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and he did not perform qunut."
"1403","1486 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, al-Aswad and Masruq, that they said: We used to pray the Fajr behind Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and he did not perform qunut."
"1404","1487 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us with this chain of his that they said: We used to pray behind Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), memorizing his bowing and prostration, and not memorizing a standing of an hour, meaning the qunut."
"1405","1488 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad and Amr ibn Maymun, they said: We prayed behind Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and he did not perform qunut in the Fajr."
"1406","1489 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, he said: I heard Ibrahim narrating from Amr ibn Maymun similar to it. Abu Ja'far said: This is contrary to what is narrated from him in the first reports. It is possible that he did each of the two matters at a time. So we examined that."
"1407","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us with that, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated to us, he said: 1490 - Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would sometimes perform qunut. Zayd informed of what we mentioned, that he would sometimes perform qunut and sometimes not perform qunut. So we wanted to examine the meaning for which he used to perform qunut, what it is?"
"1408","Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us with that, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abu Shihab al-Hannat, from Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: 1491 - Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) when he was at war would perform qunut, and when he was not at war would not perform qunut. Al-Aswad informed of the meaning for which Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to perform qunut, that when he was at war he would supplicate against his enemies, seek Allah's help against them and seek His victory, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did when those of his Companions who were killed were killed, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: You have no part in the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers. Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not supplicate against anyone after that. This verse was with Abd al-Rahman and with Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and whoever agreed with them an abrogator of supplication thereafter in the prayer against anyone. And it was not with Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) an abrogator while there was fighting; rather it abrogated, with him, supplication in the state of absence of fighting. Except that by that is established the invalidity of the saying of whoever holds continuance upon the qunut in the Fajr prayer. This is the aspect of what is narrated from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) in this chapter. As for Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), there is narrated from him in that what follows."
"1409","1492 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to perform qunut in the Subh prayer before bowing."
"1410","1493 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith and Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us. (H) 1494 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, both from Abu Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil: in the hadith of Sufyan he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and Abu Musa used to perform qunut in the morning prayer; and in the hadith of Shu'bah: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and Abu Musa performed qunut leading us."
"1411","1495 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ubayd ibn al-Hasan, he said: I heard Ibn Ma'qil say: I prayed the Subh behind Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and he performed qunut. Abu Ja'far said: It is possible that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) held the qunut in the Fajr prayer throughout, and possible that he did that at a particular time for the meaning for which Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) did it. So we examined that."
"1412","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us with that, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: 1496 - Abdullah would not perform qunut in the Fajr, and the first to perform qunut in it was Ali (may Allah be pleased with him); and they held that he only did that because he was at war."
"1413","1497 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhriz ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) only performed qunut in it here because he was at war, and he would supplicate against his enemies in the qunut in the Fajr and Maghrib. It is established by what we mentioned that the school of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) on the qunut is the school of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) which we described. And Ali did not intend by that the Fajr specifically, because he used to do that in the Maghrib in what Ibrahim mentioned."
"1414","1498 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Shu'bah, he said: Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'qil say: I prayed the Maghrib behind Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and he performed qunut and supplicated. All have agreed that the Maghrib is not performed qunut in when there is no war, and that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) only performed qunut in it because of the war; so his qunut in the Fajr also with us is likewise. As for Ibn Abbas, there is narrated from him in that what follows."
"1415","1499 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Awf, from Abu Raja', from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I prayed the Fajr with him and he performed qunut before the rak'ah."
"1416","1500 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain, with the addition that he said: This is the middle prayer. It is also possible in the affair of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) in that what was possible in the affair of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him); so we examined whether contrary to this is narrated from him."
"1417","Abu Bakrah narrated to us with that, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Waqid, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: 1501 - I prayed behind Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) and they would not perform qunut in the Subh prayer."
"1418","1502 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Za'idah informed us, from Mansur, he said: Mujahid or Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrated to us that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) would not perform qunut in the Fajr prayer."
"1419","1503 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us, from Imran ibn al-Harith al-Sulami, he said: I prayed the Subh behind Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) in his house, and he did not perform qunut before bowing nor after it."
"1420","1504 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Imran ibn al-Harith al-Sulami informed us, he said: I prayed the Subh behind Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he did not perform qunut. Abu Ja'far said: The one who narrates the qunut from him is Abu Raja', and that was only while he was in Basrah as governor over it for Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). And one of those who narrate contrary to that from him was Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and his prayer with him was only after that in Makkah; so his school in that was also the school of Umar and Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both). That which we narrated from them of the qunut in the Fajr was only from them because of the circumstance we mentioned, so they performed qunut in it and in other than it of the prayers, and abandoned that in the absence of that circumstance. And we have narrated from others of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the abandonment of the qunut throughout. Of that is what follows."
"1421","1505 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah, he said: Abdullah would not perform qunut in the Subh prayer."
"1422","1506 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from his father, he said: Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) would not perform qunut in any of the prayers except the witr, for he would perform qunut before the rak'ah."
"1423","1507 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah, he said: Abdullah would not perform qunut in the Subh prayer."
"1424","1508 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi informed us, and he mentioned the same as the hadith of Abu Bakrah from Abu Dawud from al-Mas'udi with its chain."
"1425","1509 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Mubarak narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, from al-Harith al-Ukli, from Alqamah ibn Qays, he said: I met Abu al-Darda' in Sham and asked him about the qunut, and he did not know it."
"1426","1510 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him. (H) 1511 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, from Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he would not perform qunut in any of the prayers."
"1427","1512 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr used to lead us in the Subh prayer in Makkah and would not perform qunut. Abu Ja'far said: This Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) would not perform qunut in all his life, though the Muslims were at war with their enemy in all the governorship of Umar, or in most of it, and he would not perform qunut for that; and this Abu al-Darda' denies the qunut, and Ibn al-Zubayr does not do it, though he was at war at that time, because we do not know him leading the people except at the time when the affair had come to him. These have differed with Umar ibn al-Khattab, Ali ibn Abi Talib and Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them all) in what they adopted of the qunut in the state of war, after the establishment of the removal of the qunut in the state of absence of war. When they differed in that, uncovering that by way of reasoning became necessary, to extract from the two meanings a sound meaning. What we narrated from them that they performed qunut in of the prayers for that was the Subh and Maghrib, apart from what we narrated from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to perform qunut in the Isha prayer; for in that it is also possible that it be the Maghrib, and possible that it be the last Isha. And we do not know from any of them that he performed qunut in Zuhr nor Asr in war nor otherwise. Since these two prayers have no qunut in them in war nor in absence of war, and the Fajr, Maghrib and Isha have no qunut in them in absence of war, it is established that there is no qunut in them in war also. And we have seen the witr, in it is the qunut with most of the jurists throughout, and with particular ones of them on the night of the middle of Ramadan specifically; they all only performed qunut for that prayer specifically, not for war nor otherwise. When it is negated that the qunut in what is besides it be obligatory due to the prayer specifically, not due to other than it, it is negated that it be obligatory for a meaning besides that. It is established by what we mentioned that the qunut should not be in the Fajr in war nor otherwise, by analogy and reasoning upon what we mentioned of that. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"1428","Chapter 26: What is placed first in prostration, the hands or the knees? 1513 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that when he prostrated he would begin placing his hands before his knees, and he would say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that."
"1429","1514 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur and Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, they said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hasan, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1430","1515 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hasan narrated to me, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prostrates, let him not kneel as the camel kneels, but let him place his hands then his knees. A group said: This speech is impossible, because he said: do not kneel as the camel kneels, while the camel only kneels upon its hands, then he said: but let him place his hands before his knees, so here he commanded him to do what the camel does, while in the beginning of the speech he forbade him to do what the camel does. Among the argument against them in that, in affirming this speech, establishing its soundness and negating impossibility from it, is that the camel, its knees are in its hands, and likewise in all beasts, while the children of Adam are not so; so he said: do not kneel upon your knees which are in your legs, as the camel kneels upon its knees which are in its hands, but begin placing first your hands in which there are no knees, then place your knees, so what he does in that is different from what the camel does. A group adopted that the hands are begun placing in prostration before the knees. And they argued for that with these reports. Others differed with them in that and said: rather he begins placing the knees before the hands. And they argued for that with what follows."
"1431","1516 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id, from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrated would begin with his knees before his hands. And with what follows."
"1432","1517 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id, from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prostrates, let him begin with his knees before his hands, and let him not kneel the kneeling of the stallion. This is contrary to what al-A'raj narrated from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him); and the meaning of this is: let him not kneel upon his hands as the camel kneels upon its hands."
"1433","1518 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun informed us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi, from his father, from Wa'il ibn Hujr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrated would begin placing his knees before his hands."
"1434","1519 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same; and he did not mention Wa'il. Thus Ibn Abi Dawud said from his memory: Sufyan al-Thawri, and he erred. And the correct is Shaqiq, that is Abu Layth, likewise."
"1435","1520 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us from his book, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Shaqiq Abu Layth, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father; and this Shaqiq Abu Layth is not known. When there is disagreement from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he begins placing in that, we examined that; and the way of establishing the soundness of the meanings of the reports is: that from Wa'il there is no disagreement, while the disagreement is only from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him); so what is narrated from him, when the narrations are equal in it, falls, and what Wa'il narrated is established. This is the ruling of establishing the soundness of the meanings of the reports in that. As for its aspect by way of reasoning, we have seen the limbs commanded to prostrate upon are seven limbs; by that the reports came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Of what is narrated from him in that is what follows."
"1436","1521 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The servant is commanded to prostrate upon seven bones: his face, his hands, his knees and his feet; whichever of them does not fall, there is deficiency. And with what follows."
"1437","1522 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: When the servant prostrates he prostrates upon seven bones, then he mentioned the same."
"1438","1523 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me. (H) 1524 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, from Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When the servant prostrates, seven bones prostrate with him: his face, his hands, his knees and his feet. And with what follows."
"1439","1525 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, and he mentioned the same with its chain. And with what follows."
"1440","1526 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to prostrate upon seven bones. And with what follows."
"1441","1527 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Amr, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. These limbs were those upon which prostration is. So we examined the ruling of what is agreed upon of them, to know by it the ruling of what they differed in of them. We saw the man when he prostrates begins placing one of these two, either his knees or his hands, then his head after them; and we saw him when he rises begins with his head, so the head is first in rising, last in placing, then after raising his head he follows by raising his hands then his knees. And this is agreement from them all. So reasoning upon what we described, on the ruling of the head, since it is last in placing when it is first in rising, is that the hands be likewise: since they are first before the knees in rising, they be last after them in placing. By that what Wa'il narrated is established. This is the reasoning, by it we take, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. And that has also been narrated from Umar, Abdullah and others, as follows."
"1442","1528 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim narrated to me, from the companions of Abdullah, Alqamah and al-Aswad, and they said: We memorized from Umar in his prayer that he fell after his bowing upon his knees as the camel falls, and placed his knees before his hands."
"1443","1529 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us that al-Hajjaj ibn Artah informed them, he said: Ibrahim al-Nakha'i said: It is memorized from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that his knees would fall to the ground before his hands."
"1444","1530 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mughirah, he said: I asked Ibrahim about the man who begins with his hands before his knees when he prostrates, and he said: None but a fool or a madman places that."
"1445","Chapter 27: The placing of the hands in prostration, where should it be? 1531 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abbas ibn Sahl, he said: Abu Humayd, Abu Usayd and Sahl ibn Sa'd gathered and mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrated would place his nose and forehead firmly, move his hands away from his sides, and place his palms level with his shoulders. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this and said: what befits the worshipper is to place his hands in his prostration level with his shoulders. Others differed with them in that and said: rather he places his hands in his prostration level with his ears. And they argued for that with what follows."
"1446","1532 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi, from his father, from Wa'il ibn Hujr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrated, his hands were opposite his ears."
"1447","1533 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us with that, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, he said: Asim narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"1448","1534 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us with that, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Wa'il ibn Hujr narrated to me, he said: I was a boy not comprehending the prayer of my father, so Wa'il ibn Alqamah narrated to me, from my father Wa'il ibn Hujr, he said: I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when he prostrated he would place his face between his palms."
"1449","1535 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us with that, he said: Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara', he said: I asked him where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would place his forehead when he prayed? He said: Between his palms. Every one who adopted in the raising at the opening of the prayer the shoulders also places the hands in prostration level with the shoulders; and every one who adopted in the raising at the opening of the prayer the ears also places the hands in prostration level with the ears. And it is established in what preceded of this book that the saying of whoever adopted in the raising at the opening of the prayer level with the ears is sound; so by that also the saying of whoever adopted placing the hands in prostration level with the ears is established. And it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"1450","Chapter 28: The description of sitting in prayer, how is it? 1536 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad showed them the sitting: he set up his right foot, folded his left foot, and sat upon his left hip, and did not sit upon his feet. Then he said: Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar showed me this. And he narrated to me that his father Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to do that."
"1451","1537 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar that he informed him that he saw Abdullah ibn Umar sitting cross-legged in prayer when he sat. He said: So I did it one day while I was young in age, and Abdullah ibn Umar forbade me and said: The Sunnah of prayer is only that you erect your right foot and fold the left. I said to him: But you do that. He said: My legs cannot carry me. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that sitting in the whole prayer is that a man erect his right foot and fold his left foot and sit on the ground. They argued for that with what Yahya ibn Said described in his hadith of sitting, and with the statement of Abdullah ibn Umar in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim that that is the Sunnah of prayer. They said: And Sunnah can only be from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Others disagreed with them on that and said: As for sitting at the end of prayer, it is as you mentioned; as for sitting in the first tashahhud, it is upon the left foot. And among the proof for them against what the first party argued is that the statement of Abdullah ibn Umar: the Sunnah of prayer, mentioning what is in the hadith, does not prove that it is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); it is possible he held that view or took it from someone after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hold to my Sunnah and the Sunnah of the rightly guided rightly directed caliphs after me. And Said ibn al-Musayyib, when Rabiah asked him about the blood-money of a woman's fingers, said: It is the Sunnah, O son of my brother, though its source was only from Zayd ibn Thabit; so Said called the statement of Zayd ibn Thabit Sunnah. Likewise it is possible that Abdullah ibn Umar called such a thing Sunnah even if he had nothing in it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in that is another proof: that Abdullah ibn Abdullah showed al-Qasim sitting in prayer as in his hadith, and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim reported from Abdullah ibn Abdullah, from his father, when he said to him: But you do that, and he said: My legs cannot carry me. The meaning of that was that if they could carry me I would sit upon one of them and erect the other, for his mentioning both does not indicate that one is used without the other, but both are used together: he sits upon one and erects the other. This contradicts what is in the hadith of Yahya ibn Said. And Abu Humayd al-Saidi reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that what we have already mentioned."
"1452","1538 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Humayd al-Saidi among ten of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them Abu Qatadah. He said: Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: Why? By Allah, you were neither the most attached to him among us nor the earliest of us in his companionship. He said: Yes indeed. They said: Then show us. He mentioned that in the first sitting he would fold his left leg and sit upon it, until when it was the prostration after which comes the taslim at the end, he moved his left leg back and sat mutawarrikan upon his left side. He said: They all said: You have spoken the truth. And what we have already mentioned."
"1453","1539 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi and Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Halhalah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata. 1540 - He said: And Ibn Luhayah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib and Abd al-Karim ibn al-Harith, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Abu Humayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that he did not say: They all said: You have spoken the truth."
"1454","1541 - Abu al-Husayn al-Asbahani, that is Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Makhlad, narrated to me, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Halhalah al-Duali, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. This agrees with what the people of this view held. Others also disagreed on that and said: Sitting in the whole prayer is the same, like the first sitting in the view of the people of the second position: he erects his right foot and spreads his left foot and sits upon it. They argued for that with what"
"1455","1542 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr al-Hadrami, he said: I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I shall memorize the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: When he sat for the tashahhud he spread his left foot, then sat upon it, and placed his left palm upon his left thigh, and placed his right elbow upon his right thigh, then he knotted his fingers and made a circle with the thumb and the middle finger, then he began to supplicate with the other."
"1456","1543 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Asim, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. Abu Jafar said: This agrees with what they held of that. And in the statement of Wail: Then he knotted his fingers supplicating, is proof that it was at the end of prayer. This hadith and the hadith of Abu Humayd thus contradict each other, so we examined their authenticity and the soundness of their chains."
"1457","1544 - Fahd and Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Yahya said, and Said ibn Abi Maryam, they said: Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: A man narrated to me that he found ten of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Asim exactly. Abu Jafar said: By what we mentioned, the hadith of Abu Humayd is thus undermined, for it became from Muhammad ibn Amr, from a man, and the people of isnad do not argue with the like of this. If they mention in that the weakness of Attaf ibn Khalid, it is said to them: You too declare Abd al-Hamid weaker than your declaring Attaf weak, though you do not discard the hadith of Attaf entirely; you only claim that his early hadith is all sound, and that something entered his later hadith. So Yahya ibn Main said in his book. And Abu Salih's hearing from Attaf is very early, so that enters into what Yahya deemed sound of his hadith, though Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata cannot bear the like of this. And no one makes this hadith a hearing of Muhammad ibn Amr from Abu Humayd except Abd al-Hamid, and he is weaker according to you. But the one who narrated the hadith of Abu Humayd and connected it did not detail the ruling of sitting as Abd al-Hamid detailed it."
"1458","1545 - Nasr ibn Ammar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ishkab narrated to us, he said: Abu Badr Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to me, he said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abdullah ibn Malik narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, one of the Banu Malik, from Ayyash - or Abbas - ibn Sahl al-Saidi, and he was in a gathering in which was his father, and he was among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in the gathering were Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abu Usayd and Abu Humayd al-Saidi of the Ansar, that they discussed prayer, and Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: And how? He said: I followed that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: Then show us. He said: He stood and prayed while they watched. He began and said takbir and raised his hands toward the shoulders, then said takbir for bowing and raised his hands also, then placed his hands firmly upon his knees, neither raising his head nor lowering it, then raised his head and said: Allah hears him who praises Him; O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise. Then he raised his hands, then said: Allah is Most Great, and prostrated, standing upon his palms and knees and the fronts of his feet while prostrating. Then he said takbir and sat, and tawarraka one of his legs and erected the other foot. Then he said takbir and prostrated, then said takbir and stood, and did not tawarruk. Then he returned and bowed the other rakah and said takbir likewise, then sat after two rakahs, until when he wanted to rise for standing he rose with takbir, then bowed the two rakahs, then said salam to his right: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, and said salam to his left also: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"1459","1546 - Nasr ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Badr narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr narrated to us, he said: Isa narrated to me this hadith thus, or the like of it. And of what Isa narrated also about sitting in the tashahhud is that he place his left hand upon his left thigh, and place his right hand upon his right thigh, then point in supplication with one finger."
"1460","1547 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abbas ibn Sahl, he said: Abu Humayd and Abu Usayd and Sahl ibn Sad gathered, and they mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they mentioned sitting as Abd al-Hamid mentioned in his hadith the first time, and no one besides him mentioned that."
"1461","1548 - Abu al-Husayn al-Asbahani narrated to me, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Utbah ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Adawi, from al-Abbas ibn Sahl, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, that he used to say to the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: From where? He said: I observed that from him until I memorized his prayer. He said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer he said takbir and raised his hands level with his face; when he said takbir for bowing he did the like of that; when he raised his head from bowing he said: Allah hears him who praises Him, and did the like of that, and said: Our Lord, to You is praise. When he prostrated he separated between his thighs, not resting his belly upon anything of his thighs, nor spreading his forearms. When he sat for the tashahhud he laid his left leg down and erected the right upon its front, and recited the tashahhud. This is the basis of this hadith of Abu Humayd; there is in it no mention of sitting except like what is in the hadith of Wail. And what Muhammad ibn Amr narrated is not known nor connected according to us from Abu Humayd, for in his hadith is that he was present with Abu Humayd and Abu Qatadah, while the death of Abu Qatadah was long before that, for he was killed with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed over him. So where is the age of Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata from this? Since what is connected from Abu Humayd agrees with what Wail narrated, the position with that is established and opposing it is not permitted, along with what strengthens it by way of reasoning: that we saw the first sitting in prayer and that between the two prostrations in every rakah is that he spread the left and sit upon it. Then they differed about the last sitting: it is either Sunnah or obligation. If it is Sunnah, its ruling is the ruling of the first sitting; if it is obligation, its ruling is the ruling of sitting between the two prostrations. By that, what Wail ibn Hujr narrated is established, and it is the position of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. Ibrahim al-Nakhai, may Allah have mercy on him, also held that."
"1462","1549 - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that when a man sat in prayer he used to like him to spread his left foot upon the ground, then sit upon it."
"1463","28 - Chapter of the tashahhud in prayer: how is it? 1550 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Malik ibn Anas informed me that Ibn Shihab narrated to them, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, teaching the people the tashahhud upon the minbar, saying: Say: Greetings belong to Allah, pure things belong to Allah, prayers belong to Allah; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"1464","1551 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me of the hadith of Urwah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, and he mentioned the like of it."
"1465","1552 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I said to Nafi: How did Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, recite the tashahhud? He said: He would say: In the name of Allah; greetings belong to Allah, and prayers belong to Allah, and pure things belong to Allah; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. Then he would recite the tashahhud and say: I bore witness that there is no god but Allah; I bore witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."
"1466","1553 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. 1554 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: Al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abdullah informed me, from his father, he said: When one of you recites the tashahhud, let him say. Then he mentioned the like of the tashahhud of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1467","1555 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Qasim, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to teach us the tashahhud, gesturing with her hand, then he mentioned the like of it. A group went to these hadiths and said: Such is the tashahhud in prayer, for Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, taught that to the people upon the minbar of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the presence of the Muhajirun and the Ansar, and none among them objected to him. Others disagreed with them on that and said: Had what you mentioned been binding according to the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), none of them would have opposed Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in it; yet they opposed him in it and acted contrary to it. And most of them narrated that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1468","1556 - Among those who opposed him in that is Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him: it was narrated from him on that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Wahb and Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman, from Abu Wail, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When we prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we would say: Peace be upon Allah, peace be upon Gabriel, peace be upon Michael. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to us and said: Do not say: Peace be upon Allah, for Allah is Peace; rather say: Greetings belong to Allah, and prayers and good things; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"1469","1557 - And what Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Hammad, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1470","1558 - And what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"1471","1559 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"1472","1560 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhill ibn Muhriz al-Dabbi narrated to us. 1561 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Muhill ibn Muhriz narrated to us, he said: Shaqiq narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. And Husayn added in his hadith: They said: And they would learn it as one of you learns a surah of the Quran."
"1473","1562 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Habib narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from Abdullah, that he said: I took the tashahhud from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he taught it to me word by word. Then he mentioned the tashahhud that is in the hadith of Abu Wail. And he added, he said: They would conceal the tashahhud and not utter it loudly."
"1474","1563 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Mughirah al-Dabbi narrated to us, he said: Shaqiq ibn Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Hammad and Mansur and Sulayman and Muhill from Abu Wail, except that he did not say: And His blessings."
"1475","1564 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. 1565 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. 1566 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil informed us, both from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: We did not know what to say between every two rakahs except glorifying Allah and magnifying Him and praising our Lord, mighty and majestic, and that Muhammad was taught the openings of speech and its conclusions - or he said: its comprehensive phrases - and he said: When one of you sits in the two rakahs, let him say. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"1476","1567 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar and Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Al-Masudi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us the khutbah of prayer, and he mentioned the like of it. Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, also disagreed with him on that: it was narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that what"
"1477","Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us. He said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth and Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Said ibn Jubayr and Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us the tashahhud as he taught us the Quran; he would say: Blessed greetings, prayers, good things belong to Allah; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."
"1478","1568 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim informed me, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Ata was asked, while I listened, about the tashahhud, and he said: Blessed greetings, good things, prayers belong to Allah. Then he mentioned the like of it. Then he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr saying them upon the minbar, teaching them to the people, and I heard Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, saying the like of what I heard Ibn al-Zubayr say. I said: Did Ibn al-Zubayr and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, not differ? He said: No. Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, also disagreed with him on that."
"1479","1569 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Babi al-Makki narrated to me, he said: I prayed beside Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, and when he finished his prayer he struck his hand upon my thigh and said: Shall I not teach you the greeting of prayer as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us? He said: He recited these words like what is in the hadith of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1480","1570 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Yahya ibn Ismail al-Baghdadi at Tabariyyah narrated to us, they said: Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr - Ibn Abi Dawud said in his hadith: from Mujahid, and Yahya said: I heard Mujahid - narrating from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the tashahhud: Greetings belong to Allah, prayers, good things; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. Except that Yahya added in his hadith: Ibn Umar said: I added in it: And His blessings, and I added in it: Alone, without partner."
"1481","1571 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, he said: I used to circumambulate with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, around the House while he taught me the tashahhud, saying: Greetings belong to Allah, prayers, good things; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: And I added in it: And His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: And I added in it: Alone, without partner; and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. 1572 - And thus Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Muadh, from his father, from Shubah, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that the statement of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, in it: And I added in it, indicates that he took that from someone else besides himself, either the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1482","1573 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, used to teach us the tashahhud upon the minbar as you teach children writing, then he mentioned the like of the tashahhud of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, exactly. This that we narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, contradicts what Salim and Nafi narrated from him, and this is more worthy, for he related it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and taught it to Mujahid; it is impossible that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, would leave what he took from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for what he took from someone else. Abu Said al-Khudri disagreed with him on that: it was narrated from him on that what"
"1483","1574 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Yusuf al-Anmati narrated to us - Ibn Abi Dawud, a trustworthy Basran, said - he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We would learn the tashahhud as we learn a surah of the Quran, then he mentioned the like of the tashahhud of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, exactly. Jabir ibn Abdullah also disagreed with him on that: it was narrated from him on that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what"
"1484","1575 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Ayman ibn Nabil narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us the tashahhud as he taught us a surah of the Quran: In the name of Allah and by Allah. Then he mentioned the like of the tashahhud of Ibn Masud exactly, except that he said: His servant and His Messenger, and I ask Allah for Paradise, and I seek refuge with Allah from the Fire. Abu Musa al-Ashari also disagreed with him on that: it was narrated from him on that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we have already mentioned:"
"1485","1576 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi, he said: I heard Abu Musa al-Ashari say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and taught us our prayer and clarified our Sunnah for us, and he said: When it is in the second sitting, let the statement of one of you be: Greetings, good things, prayers belong to Allah; peace - or he said: peace, Said doubted - be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"1486","1577 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghallab Yunus ibn Jubayr narrated to us that Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi narrated to him, he said: Abu Musa al-Ashari said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and taught us our Sunnah and taught us our prayer, and he said: When it is at the sitting, let the statement of one of you be: Greetings, good things, prayers belong to Allah; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr also disagreed with him on that: it was narrated from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that what we have already mentioned:"
"1487","1578 - Muhammad ibn Humayd Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah informed me, he said: Al-Harith ibn Yazid narrated to me that Abu Aslam the muadhhdhin narrated to him that he heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr say: The tashahhud of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with which he used to recite tashahhud is: In the name of Allah and by Allah, the best of names; greetings, good things, prayers belong to Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger; He sent him with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner, and that the Hour is coming, no doubt about it; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; O Allah, forgive me and guide me. All of these narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the tashahhud what we mentioned from them, contradicting what was narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrations from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that are successive, nothing contradicts them, so they should not be opposed nor should other than them be taken, nor should anything in them be added to, except that in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, is a word extra over others, and it is:"
"1488","The blessed. Some said: It is more worthy than the hadith of others, since he added over it, and the one who adds is more worthy than the one who omits. Others said: Rather the hadith of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abu Musa and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, which Mujahid and Ibn Babi narrated from him, is more worthy, due to the soundness of their chains and their agreement upon that, for Abu al-Zubayr does not equal al-Amash, nor Mansur, nor Mughirah, nor their likes among those who narrated the hadith of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, nor does he equal Qatadah in the hadith of Abu Musa, nor Abu Bishr in the hadith of Ibn Umar. Had taking what is added been obligatory, even if below them, then taking what Ibn Nabil added over al-Layth from Abu al-Zubayr would be obligatory, for he said in the tashahhud also: In the name of Allah; and taking what Abu Aslam added from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr would be obligatory, for he said in the tashahhud also: In the name of Allah, and added also over what is in that of addition over the hadith of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them. Since this addition is not accepted, because none like him added it over al-Layth, the addition of Ibn Abi al-Zubayr in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, over Ata ibn Abi Rabah is not accepted, for Ibn Jurayj narrated it from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, as mawquf; and Abu al-Zubayr narrated it from Said ibn Jubayr and Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, as marfu. Had all these hadiths been established and equal in their chains, the hadith of Abdullah would be the worthiest of them, for they agreed that a man may not recite tashahhud with whatever tashahhud he wishes other than what was narrated of that. Since it is established that the tashahhud is with specific remembrance, and what Abdullah narrated was agreed to by all who narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides him, while others added over it what is not in his tashahhud, what was agreed upon of that is worthier to recite tashahhud with than what was differed over. And another proof is that we saw Abdullah was strict in that, to the point he held his companions to the waw in it, so they would agree with the wording of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we know of no one else who did that. For this we preferred what was narrated from Abdullah over what was narrated from others. Among what was narrated from Abdullah on what we mentioned is what"
"1489","1579 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah used to hold us to the waw in the tashahhud."
"1490","1580 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, he said: Abdullah heard a man saying in the tashahhud: In the name of Allah, greetings belong to Allah, and Abdullah said to him: Do you eat?"
"1491","1581 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that al-Rabi ibn Khaytham met Alqamah and said: It has occurred to me to add in the tashahhud: and His forgiveness. Alqamah said to him: We stop at what we were taught."
"1492","29 - Chapter of the salam in prayer: how is it? 1583 - Rabi al-Jizi and Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn Abi Bakr al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Musab ibn Thabit, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, from Amir ibn Sad, from Sad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give salam at the end of prayer with one taslimah: Peace be upon you. Abu Jafar said: A group went to the view that the praying person gives one salam in his prayer facing forward: Peace be upon you. They argued for that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them on that and said: Rather he should give salam to his right and to his left, saying in each of the two taslims: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah. And among the proof for them against the people of the first position is that this hadith of Sad was narrated as he mentioned only by al-Darawardi in particular, and all who narrated it from Musab besides him differed with him on that."
"1493","1584 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Musab ibn Thabit narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, from Amir ibn Sad, from Sad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, until the whiteness of his cheeks could be seen from here and from here."
"1494","1585 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Musab ibn Thabit, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. This Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak, with his memory and precision, narrated it from Musab contrary to what al-Darawardi narrated from him, and Muhammad ibn Amr agreed with him on that, despite his precedence and eminence. Then this hadith was narrated from Ismail ibn Muhammad from other than Musab as Muhammad ibn Amr and Ibn al-Mubarak narrated it, not as al-Darawardi narrated it."
"1495","1586 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us. 1587 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, from Amir ibn Sad, from Sad, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give salam to his right until I saw the whiteness of his cheek, and to his left until I saw the whiteness of his cheek. By what we mentioned, what al-Darawardi narrated from him is negated, and it is established from Sad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to give two taslims. And more than one of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreed with him on that."
"1496","1588 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Burayd ibn Abi Maryam, from Abu Musa, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in prayer on the day of the Camel, a prayer that reminded us of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - whether we had forgotten it or deliberately left it - and he would say takbir in every lowering and rising, and give salam to his right and to his left."
"1497","1589 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give salam to his right and to his left until the whiteness of his cheek appeared: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"1498","1590 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1499","1591 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Alqamah and al-Aswad ibn Yazid and Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1500","1592 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1501","1593 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, used to give salam to their rights and to their lefts in prayer: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"1502","1594 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah. 1595 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us. 1596 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Jawwab al-Ahwas ibn Jawwab narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, and Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, the like of it."
"1503","1597 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, and Mansur, from Mujahid, from Abu Mamar, from Abdullah, he said: An amir prayed in Makkah and gave salam to his right and to his left, and Abdullah said: From where did he pick it up? Al-Hakam said in his hadith: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it."
"1504","1598 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1505","1599 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Ammar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give salam in his prayer to his right and to his left."
"1506","1600 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yahya al-Mazini informed me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi ibn Habban, that he asked Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: He would say takbir whenever he lowered and raised, and give salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"1507","1601 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give two taslims in prayer, to his right and to his left."
"1508","1602 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us. 1603 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Qibtiyyah, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: When we prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we would give salam with our hands, saying: Peace be upon you, peace be upon you. He said: What is with people that they give salam with their hands as if they are the tails of restive horses? Does it not suffice one of you when he sits in prayer to place his hand upon his thigh and point with his finger, and say: Peace be upon you, peace be upon you."
"1509","1604 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ibrahim al-Tarjuman narrated to us, he said: Hudayj ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give two taslims in prayer."
"1510","1605 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad and Abu al-Rabi narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Hurayth, from al-Shabi, from al-Bara, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1511","1606 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. 1607 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, he said: I heard Hujr Abu al-Anbas narrating from Wail ibn Hujr that he prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he gave salam to his right and to his left."
"1512","1608 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman narrating from Wail ibn Hujr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1513","1609 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I read to al-Fudayl: Abu Hariz narrated to me that Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated to him that Adi ibn Amirah al-Hadrami narrated to him, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salam in prayer he turned his face to his right until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen, then he would give salam to his left, turning his face until the whiteness of his left cheek could be seen."
"1514","1610 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Budayl narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, he said: Abu Malik al-Ashari said to his people: Shall I not pray for you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He mentioned the prayer and gave salam to his right and to his left, then said: Such was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1515","1611 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Mulazim ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Hawdhah ibn Qays ibn Talq narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather Talq ibn Ali, he said: When we prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he gave salam, we saw the whiteness of his right cheek and the whiteness of his left cheek."
"1516","1612 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Umayr ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah al-Taifi, from Aws ibn Aws, or Aws ibn Abi Aws, he said: I stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) half a month, and I saw him pray and give salam to his right and to his left."
"1517","1613 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Sufi narrated to us, he said: Ashath ibn Shubah narrated to us, he said: al-Minhal ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, he said: Abu Rimthah led us in prayer, then narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave salam in prayer to his right and to his left. Abu Jafar said: We know nothing sound from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the salam in prayer except what has entered into what we narrated in this chapter. Whoever opposes that opposes it only toward the hadith of al-Darawardi whose corruption we have explained at the beginning of this chapter. A group also argued on that with what"
"1518","1614 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah would give one taslimah. It was said to them: This is a hadith whose basis is mawquf upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her; thus the memorizers narrated it. And Zuhayr ibn Muhammad, though a trustworthy man, the narration of Amr ibn Abi Salamah from him is very weak. So Yahya ibn Main said, as more than one of our companions related from him to me - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah conveyed them to me - claiming that in them is much confusion. If one says: Since it is established from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, as you mentioned, with whom do you counter her among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? It is said to him: With Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them; we have narrated that from them in what preceded of this chapter. And:"
"1519","1615 - Husayn ibn Nasr and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hammad, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, used to give salam to his right and to his left, then move immediately, as if upon hot coals."
"1520","1616 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Wahb narrated to us, they said: Shubah and Hisham narrated to us. 1617 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Hammad, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1521","1618 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Razin, he said: I prayed behind Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and he gave salam to his right and to his left."
"1522","1619 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Razin, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, used to give salam to his right and to his left. It was said to Sufyan: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: Yes."
"1523","1620 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Razin, he said: I prayed behind Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abdullah, and they both gave two taslims."
"1524","1621 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to give salam in prayer to his right and to his left."
"1525","1622 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, that he prayed behind Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ibn Masud, and both of them gave salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"1526","1623 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Shaqiq, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to give salam in prayer to his right and to his left."
"1527","1624 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, that an amir prayed in Makkah and gave two taslims, and Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do you see from where he picked it up? I heard Ibn Abi Dawud say: Yahya ibn Main said: This is among the soundest of what was narrated in this chapter."
"1528","1625 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, he said: Ammar was our amir for a year; he did not pray a prayer except he gave salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"1529","1626 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to me, from his father, that he saw Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, when he finished prayer, give salam to his right and to his left. Abu Jafar said: These are the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Abu Bakr and Umar and Ali and Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them, and Ammar, and those we mentioned with them - giving salam to their rights and to their lefts, with no one else objecting to them, despite their time being close to seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and memorizing his actions. No one should oppose them even had nothing been narrated on that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). How then when there has been narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees with their action, may Allah be pleased with them? If one denies what we narrated from Abu Wail from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to give two taslims in prayer, and what we narrated from him on that from Abdullah, and argues for what he denied of that with what"
"1530","1627 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. 1628 - And with what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I said to Abu Wail: Do you remember the takbir? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Then the taslim? He said: One. He said: How then is it permissible that he remembers the taslim as one while he saw Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abdullah giving two? Do you think he remembered the one from other than them, while from them it was memorized and them it was followed? In the establishment of this from him is what necessitates the corruption of what you narrated from him about the two taslims. It was said to him: What we narrated from him about the two taslims is sound; nothing entered its isnad nor its text. And that is about the salam from the prayers with bowing and prostration. What Abu Wail intended in the hadith of Amr ibn Murrah of salam once is about prayer with takbir only, for a group of the Kufans, among them Ibrahim, used to give in their funeral prayer one concealed taslimah, and give in their other prayers two taslims. Such is the meaning of the hadith of Abu Wail according to us on that, and this is worthier to carry what was narrated from him on that upon, so that parts of it do not contradict others. If one says: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin used to give in their prayer one taslimah, and he mentioned on that what we have already mentioned:"
"1531","1629 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Muadh narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, and from Ashath, from al-Hasan, that both of them used to give salam in prayer with one taslimah facing forward."
"1532","1630 - And what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from al-Hasan and Muhammad: one taslimah."
"1533","1631 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Said, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, the like of it. It was said to him: You spoke truly; this was narrated from them. And there was narrated from those before them among those we mentioned what contradicts that, along with what is successive from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we already mentioned in this chapter. And there was narrated from Said ibn al-Musayyib and Ibn Abi Layla, both Tabiis greater than those, contrary to what was narrated from them."
"1534","1632 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub informed me, from Zahrah ibn Mabad, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib used to give salam to his right and to his left."
"1535","1633 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, he said: I used to pray with Ibn Abi Layla, and he would give salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah. These two are Tabiis with precedence and companionship with a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that those who oppose them among those we mentioned in this chapter do not have. What we narrated from them of that is worthier, due to their following those before them, and their agreement with what is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that. This too is the position of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"1536","30 - Chapter of the salam in prayer: is it among its obligations or its Sunan? 1634 - Al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The key of prayer is purification, its entering is the takbir, and its exiting is the taslim. A group went to the view that when a man leaves his prayer without taslim his prayer is void, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Its exiting is the taslim, so it is not permitted to exit from it by other than it. Others disagreed with them on that and split into two positions. Among them are those who said: When he sits the measure of the tashahhud, his prayer is complete even if he does not give salam. And among them are those who said: When he raises his head from the last prostration of his prayer, his prayer is complete even if he does not recite tashahhud and does not give salam. And among the proof for both parties against the people of the first position is that what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his statement: Its exiting is the taslim, was only narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. And there was narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of his own view on the like of that what indicates that the meaning of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) according to him was other than what the people of the first position carried it upon. They mentioned what we have already mentioned:"
"1537","1635 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from al-Hakam, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When he raises his head from the last prostration his prayer is complete. This Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Its exiting is the taslim, yet according to him that was not that prayer is complete only with taslim, since according to him it was complete with what precedes taslim. And the meaning of Its exiting is the taslim according to him was also the exiting with which one should exit, not with other than it; and the completion after which whatever occurs does not require repeating the prayer is other than it. If one says: He said: Its entering is the takbir, and it was that by which one enters only with it; likewise when he said: And its exiting is the taslim, it is like it also: one exits from it only with it. It is said to him: One may not enter things except from where one was commanded to enter them, while one may exit from things from where one was commanded to exit from them and from other than that. Of that is that we saw marriage: it was forbidden to contract it with a woman in her waiting period, and whoever contracted it with her while she was so did not thereby own her, nor was marriage with her due to him - with many likes of that whose mention would lengthen the book. And one was commanded not to exit from it except with divorce in which there is no sin, with the divorced woman being pure without intercourse. Whoever divorced contrary to what he was commanded of that, divorcing three times or divorcing his wife menstruating, that binds him though it is sinful, and he exits by that prohibited divorce from the valid marriage. So the causes by which private parts are owned are established as they are, and the causes by which ownerships of them cease are established as they are, and they were forbidden what contradicts that or anything of it. Whoever did what he was forbidden of that to enter with it into marriage did not enter with it into it, while when he did anything of it to exit with it from marriage he exited with it from it. Since one enters things only from where one was commanded, while exiting from them may be from where one was commanded and may be by other than that, so it is likewise by reasoning about prayer that it be so: entering it is obligatory only with what one was commanded of entering it, while exiting from it is with what one was commanded of what one exits with from it, and with other than that. And among what those who held that when he raises his head from the last prostration of his prayer his prayer is complete argued with is what"
"1538","1636 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi and Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When he raises his head from the last prostration, his prayer has passed if he breaks his purity. And what"
"1539","1637 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi al-Lului narrated to us, they said: Muadh ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. It was said to them: This hadith was differed over: a group narrated it thus, and others narrated it otherwise."
"1540","1638 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi al-Tanukhi and Bakr ibn Sawadah al-Judhami, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the imam finishes prayer and sits, then he or one of those who completed prayer with him breaks purity before the imam gives salam, his prayer is complete, and he does not return to it. Abu Jafar said: This has a meaning other than the first hadith. And this hadith was also narrated with a wording other than this."
"1541","1639 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Bakrah, from Abu Dawud, from Ibn al-Mubarak."
"1542","1640 - Muadh said: I met Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum, and he narrated to me from Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi and Bakr ibn Sawadah. I said to him: Did you meet both of them? He said: Both of them narrated it to me from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the praying person raises his head from the end of his prayer and finishes his tashahhud, then breaks purity, his prayer is complete, and he does not return to it. Those who said: Prayer is not complete until one sits in it the measure of the tashahhud, argued with what"
"1543","1641 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym and Abu Ghassan narrated to us - the wording is Abu Nuaym's - they said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr, he said: Al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah narrated to me, he said: Alqamah took my hands and narrated to me that Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, took his hand, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his hand and taught him the tashahhud. He mentioned the tashahhud as we mentioned from Abdullah in the chapter of the tashahhud. And he said: When you do that or complete this, your prayer is complete; if you wish to stand then stand, and if you wish to sit then sit."
"1544","1642 - Al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1545","1643 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Mashar al-Barra narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the tashahhud, and said: There is no prayer except with tashahhud. They narrated what we mentioned of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then they narrated of the statement of Abdullah what"
"1546","1644 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Waki narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: The tashahhud is the ending of prayer, and the taslim is permission of its ending. Then there was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that leaving the salam does not invalidate prayer, and it is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr five rakahs and did not give salam; when he was informed of what he did, he folded his leg and prostrated two prostrations. As"
"1547","1645 - Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that. In this hadith is that he added into prayer a rakah not of it before the salam, and did not consider that invalidating prayer; had he considered it invalidating he would have repeated it. Since he did not repeat it, though he had exited from it to the fifth, not by taslim, that proves the salam is not of the substance of prayer. Do you not see that had he come with the fifth while a prostration of what preceded remained upon him, that would invalidate the four, for he mixed them with what is not of them? Had the salam been obligatory like the obligation of the prostration of prayer, its ruling would likewise be so; but it is contrary to it, so it is Sunnah. And there was also narrated in the hadith of Abu Said al-Khudri that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays and does not know whether he prayed three or four, let him build upon certainty and leave doubt. If his prayer was deficient he has completed it, and the two prostrations humiliate Satan; if his prayer was complete, what he added and the two prostrations are voluntary for him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thus made the added fifth and the two prostrations of forgetfulness voluntary, and did not make what preceded of prayer thereby invalid, though the praying person had exited from it to it. By that it is established that prayer is complete without taslim, and that taslim is among its Sunan, not its substance. Correcting the meanings of the reports in this chapter thus requires what those who said: Prayer is not complete until one sits the measure of the tashahhud, held; for the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bore what we mentioned, and the hadith of Abdullah ibn Amr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was differed over as we described, while the hadith of Ibn Masud is what was not differed over. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: those who said that when he raises his head from the last prostration of his prayer his prayer is complete said: We saw this sitting, the sitting of tashahhud, in it remembrance recited as tashahhud and taslim exiting with it from prayer; and we saw before it in prayer a sitting in it remembrance recited as tashahhud. All agreed that that first sitting and what is in it of remembrance is not of the substance of prayer, but is among its Sunan. And the last sitting was differed over. By reasoning upon what we mentioned it should be like the first sitting, and what is in it like what is in the first sitting: Sunnah, and all done in it Sunnah, as the first sitting was Sunnah and all done in it was Sunnah. And we saw the standing in all prayer and the bowing and prostration in it likewise all so; by reasoning upon what we mentioned the sitting in it should likewise all be so. Since part of it by their agreement is Sunnah, the rest of it is likewise so by reasoning. The others argued against them and said: We saw one who stands from the first sitting forgetfully until he stands fully is commanded to proceed in his standing and not commanded to return to sitting; and we saw one who stands from the last sitting forgetfully until he stands fully is commanded to return to his sitting. They said: What one is commanded to return to after standing from it is the obligation, and what one is not commanded to return to after standing from it is not obligation. Do you not see that one who stands while a prostration of his prayer remains upon him until he stands fully is commanded to return to what he stood from, for he stood leaving an obligation and was commanded to return to it? Likewise the last sitting: since one who stood from it is commanded to return to it, that proves it is obligation; had it been other than obligation he would not have been commanded to return to it, as he was not commanded to return to the first sitting. Among the proof for the others against them is that one who stood from the first sitting until he stood fully was only commanded to proceed in his standing and not return to his sitting because he stood from a sitting that is not obligation and entered into a standing that is obligation, so he was not commanded to leave the obligation and return to other than obligation, but commanded to continue upon the obligation until he completes it. Had he stood from the first sitting and not stood fully he would be commanded to return to sitting, for so long as he has not stood fully he has not entered into obligation, so he was commanded to return from what is neither Sunnah nor obligation to the sitting that is Sunnah. And one is commanded to return from what is neither Sunnah nor obligation to what is Sunnah, and commanded to return from Sunnah to what is obligation. And one who stood from the last sitting until he stood fully entered into neither Sunnah nor obligation, having stood from a sitting that is Sunnah, so he was commanded to return to it and leave continuing in what is neither Sunnah nor obligation - as one who stood from the first sitting that is Sunnah and did not stand fully so as to enter obligation was commanded to return from that to the sitting that is Sunnah. For this, one who stood from the last sitting until he stood fully was commanded to return to it, not for what the others held. Abu Jafar said: This is the reasoning according to us in this chapter, not what the others said. But Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, went in that to the position of those who said: The last sitting the measure of the tashahhud is of the substance of prayer, for it is established by text as we mentioned. And some of the predecessors held what they held of that. As"
"1548","1646 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, about a man who breaks purity after raising his head from the last prostration: He said: It does not suffice him until he recites tashahhud or sits the measure of the tashahhud."
"1549","1647 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sabiq al-Rashidi narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata used to say: When a man finishes the last tashahhud and says: Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah, then breaks purity even if he has not given salam to his right and to his left - he mentioned speech whose meaning is: His prayer has passed; or he said: He does not return to it."
"1550","31 - Chapter of the witr. 1648 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me. 1649 - And Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, he said: I heard Abu Mijlaz narrating from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The witr is one rakah at the end of the night."
"1551","1650 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Abu Mijlaz, and he mentioned the like of it."
"1552","1651 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, about the witr, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: One rakah at the end of the night. And I asked Ibn Umar, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One rakah at the end of the night. Abu Jafar said: A group went to this, followed it, and made it a basis. Others disagreed with them on that and split into two parties: some said: The witr is three rakahs, giving salam only at the end of them; and some said: The witr is three rakahs, giving salam in two of them and at the end of them. And the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The witr is one rakah at the end of the night, may bear according to us what the people of the first position said, and may be one rakah after preceding even rakahs, all of that being witr, so that rakah makes odd the preceding even. And that was clarified by what some of them narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them."
"1553","1652 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about night prayer, and he said: Two by two; when you fear dawn, pray one rakah making odd your prayer."
"1554","1653 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi and Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1555","1654 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1556","1655 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1557","1656 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1558","1657 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1559","1658 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Habib, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1560","1659 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed me, he said: Abdullah ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1561","1660 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Tawus, he said: I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1562","1661 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah and Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1563","1662 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah and Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them - both informed them - from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1564","1663 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim and Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman - both narrated to him - from Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1565","1664 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Bahr al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Wadin ibn Ata, he said: Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar informed me, from Ibn Umar, that he used to separate between his even and his witr with a taslimah, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, informed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that. He thus informed that he would pray even and witr, all of that in sum being witr. And his statement: He separates with a taslimah, may mean by that taslimah the tashahhud, and may mean the taslim that ends prayer."
"1566","1665 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, that Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, used to give salam between the rakah and the two rakahs in the witr, until he would order some of his need."
"1567","1666 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, from Bakr ibn Abdullah, he said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, prayed two rakahs, then said: Boy, saddle for us. Then he stood and made witr with one rakah. In these reports is that he would make witr with three, but he would separate between the one and the two. It is thus agreed from him about the witr that it is three. And there came from him of his own view also what indicates that the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned is as we described, bearing interpretation."
"1568","1667 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Uqbah ibn Muslim, he said: I asked Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, about the witr, and he said: Do you know the witr of the day? I said: Yes, the Maghrib prayer. He said: You spoke truly, or: well. Then he said: While we were in the mosque a man stood and asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the witr, or about night prayer, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Night prayer is two by two; when you fear dawn, make witr with one. Do you not see that Ibn Umar, when Uqbah asked him about the witr, said: Do you know the witr of the day? That is: it is like it. And in that is what tells you that the witr according to Ibn Umar was three like the Maghrib prayer, since he made his answer to his questioner about the witr of the night: Do you know the witr of the day, the Maghrib prayer? Then he narrated to him after that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we mentioned. It is thus established that his statement: Make witr with one, means: with something preceding it, making odd with that one what you prayed before it, and all of that is witr. And that was also clarified with what"
"1569","1668 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Amir al-Shabi, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them: How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at night? They said: Thirteen rakahs: eight, and he makes witr with three, and two rakahs after Fajr. Thus in the copies."
"1570","1669 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah al-Makhzumi narrated to me that a man asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, about the witr, and he ordered him to separate. The man said: I fear people will say it is al-butayra. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: Do you want the Sunnah of Allah and the Sunnah of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? This is the Sunnah of Allah and the Sunnah of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there was narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in her mentioning the witr of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates the truth of what we mentioned."
"1571","1670 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Sad ibn Hisham, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not give salam in the two rakahs of the witr."
"1572","1671 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Said, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. She thus informed that the witr is three, giving no salam between any of them. Then there were narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, after this, hadiths about the witr which, when uncovered, return to the meaning of this hadith of Sad."
"1573","1672 - Among that is what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah informed me, he said: Al-Hasan narrated to us, from Sad ibn Hisham, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at night he opened his prayer with two light rakahs, then prayed eight rakahs, then made witr. She thus informed here that he would pray two, then eight, then make witr. The meaning of: Then make witr, bears: Then make witr with three, of them two rakahs of the eight and a rakah after them, so all he prayed is eleven rakahs. And it bears: Then make witr with three consecutive, so all he prayed is thirteen rakahs. We examined what that bears, whether anything came indicating any of it specifically."
"1574","1673 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us: Husayn ibn Nafi al-Anbari narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Sad ibn Hisham, he said: I entered upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: Narrate to me about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray at night eight rakahs and make witr with the ninth; when he became bulky he prayed six rakahs and made witr with the seventh, and prayed two rakahs sitting. In this hadith is that he would make witr with the ninth. That bears that he would make witr with the ninth along with two of the eight before it, so that this hadith and the hadith of Zurarah agree and do not contradict."
"1575","1674 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Sad ibn Hisham al-Ansari, that he asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at night, and she said: He would pray Isha, then lighten with two rakahs, having prepared his siwak and his purification; Allah would wake him for what He willed to wake him for, and he would use the siwak and perform ablution, then pray two rakahs, then stand and pray eight rakahs, equalizing them in recitation, then make witr with the ninth. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aged and took on flesh, he made those eight six, then would make witr with the seventh, then pray two rakahs sitting, reciting in them: Say, O disbelievers, and: When the earth is shaken. In this hadith is that he would pray before the eight with which he makes odd with their ninth, four; all of that is thirteen rakahs, of them the witr which Zurarah explained, from Sad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and it is three rakahs, giving salam only at the end of them. The narration of Sad from Aishah is thus sound and firm upon what we mentioned."
"1576","1675 - And Abdullah ibn Shaqiq narrated from Aishah on that what Rabi al-Muadhhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hushaym ibn Bashir narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhhdha informed me, he said: Abdullah ibn Shaqiq informed me, he said: I asked Aishah about the voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at night, and she said: When he prayed Isha with the people he would enter and pray two rakahs. She said: And he would pray at night nine rakahs, in them the witr; when dawn rose he would pray two rakahs in my house, then go out and lead the people in Fajr prayer. In this hadith is that he would pray, when he entered his house after Isha, two rakahs, and at night nine, in them the witr. That, according to us, is nine besides the two rakahs which he would lighten, upon what Sad ibn Hisham said from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would open his night prayer with two light rakahs. We only carried the meaning of the hadith of Abdullah ibn Shaqiq upon this meaning so that it and the hadith of Sad ibn Hisham agree and do not contradict."
"1577","1676 - And Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, on that what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray at night thirteen rakahs: pray eight rakahs, then make witr with a rakah, then pray two rakahs sitting; when he wanted to bow he stood and bowed, and prayed between the adhan of Fajr and the iqamah two rakahs. It is possible that the eight rakahs with whose ninth he made witr in this hadith are the eight rakahs which Sad ibn Hisham mentioned from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray before them four rakahs, so that this hadith and the hadith of Sad agree, and this hadith would have added over the hadith of Sad and the hadith of Abdullah ibn Shaqiq the voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the witr. And it is also possible that these nine are the nine which Sad ibn Hisham mentioned in his hadith from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray them when he became bulky, so that is nine rakahs with the two light rakahs with which he would open his prayer; then he would pray after the witr two rakahs sitting in place of what he would pray before becoming bulky standing, namely two rakahs. That thus returns also to thirteen rakahs."
"1578","1677 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, he said: I asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at night, and she said: He would pray thirteen rakahs: pray eight rakahs, then pray two rakahs sitting; when he wanted to bow he stood and bowed standing, then prostrated; and he would pray two rakahs between the adhan and the iqamah of Subh prayer. This hadith has the meaning of the hadith of Ahmad ibn Dawud from Sahl, except that he omitted mention of the witr."
"1579","1678 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray at night eleven rakahs, of them two rakahs sitting, and pray two rakahs before Subh; that is thirteen rakahs. This hadith also agreed with the hadith of Ahmad ibn Dawud. And her statement: He prays two rakahs before Subh, means before Subh prayer, namely the two rakahs which Ahmad ibn Dawud mentioned in his hadith that he would pray them between the adhan and the iqamah."
"1580","1679 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Al-Qawariri narrated to us. 1680 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Labid narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Salamah say: I entered upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at night, and she said: His prayer in Ramadan and otherwise was thirteen rakahs, of them the two rakahs of Fajr. This hadith also agreed with what we narrated before it of the hadiths of Abu Salamah."
"1581","1681 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he informed him that he asked Aishah: How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Ramadan? She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not exceed in Ramadan nor otherwise eleven rakahs: pray four, so do not ask about their beauty and length; then pray four, so do not ask about their beauty and length; then pray three. Aishah said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you sleep before making witr? He said: O Aishah, my eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep. This hadith bears that her statement: Then pray three, means making odd with one of them two of the eight, then praying the remaining two rakahs - namely the two rakahs which Abu Salamah mentioned in what preceded of what we narrated from him that he would pray them sitting - so that this hadith and the preceding of his hadiths agree. And it bears that the three are all witr, and this is the more likely of the two meanings, for she detailed his prayer: she said: He would pray four, then four, describing all of that with beauty and length; then she said: Then pray three, without describing that with length, and she joined the three in mention. That according to us is upon the witr. All he would pray is thus eleven rakahs with the two light rakahs in the hadith of Sad ibn Hisham, or with the two rakahs which he would pray sitting after the witr. This is likelier with the narrations of Abu Salamah, for all of them inform of his prayer after he became bulky, while the hadith of Sad ibn Hisham informs of his prayer after he became bulky and of his prayer before that."
"1582","1682 - And Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, on that what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray at night eleven rakahs and make witr of them with one; when he finished them he would lie upon his right side until the muadhhdhin came to him, then pray two light rakahs. This bears that it is upon his prayer before becoming bulky, so that is all he would pray with the two light rakahs with which he would open his prayer. And it bears that it is upon his prayer after he became bulky, so that is eleven: nine of them, in them the witr, and two rakahs after them sitting, upon what is in the hadith of Abu Salamah, and upon what is in the hadith of Sad ibn Hisham and Abdullah ibn Shaqiq. Except that other than Malik narrated this hadith and added in it something."
"1583","1683 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus and Amr ibn al-Harith and Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Ibn Shihab - they informed them - from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray between finishing Isha prayer and Fajr eleven rakahs, giving salam between every two rakahs and making witr with one; and prostrate a prostration the measure of what one of you recites fifty verses; when the muadhhdhin fell silent after Fajr prayer and dawn became clear to him, he stood and bowed two light rakahs, then lay upon his right side until the muadhhdhin came to him for the iqamah, and he would go out with him. And some of them add over others in the story of the hadith."
"1584","1684 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. In this hadith is that all he would pray after the last Isha until Fajr is eleven rakahs. That thus returns to the hadith of Abu Salamah, and we knew by it that that prayer is his prayer after he became bulky. As for her statement: Giving salam between every two rakahs, that bears that he would give salam between every two rakahs in the witr and otherwise, establishing thereby what the people of Madinah hold of giving salam between the even and the witr. And it bears that he would give salam between every two rakahs of that besides the witr, so that it and the hadith of Sad ibn Hisham agree and do not contradict - though there was narrated from Urwah on this contrary to what al-Zuhri narrated from him. Among that is what"
"1585","1685 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray at night thirteen rakahs, then pray when he heard the call two light rakahs. This contradicts what is in the hadith of Ibn Abi Dhib and Amr and Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah. That bears that the two extra rakahs in this hadith over that hadith are the two light rakahs which Sad ibn Hisham mentioned in his hadith, and there is in that no proof of how his witr was."
"1586","1686 - We examined that, and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make witr with five prostrations, meaning rakahs."
"1587","1687 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make witr with five prostrations, not sitting between them until he sat in the fifth, then giving salam."
"1588","1688 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed me, from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make witr with five, sitting only at the end of them. What Hisham and Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated from Urwah thus contradicted what al-Zuhri narrated of his statement: He would pray eleven rakahs, making witr of them with one, and giving salam between every two rakahs. Since what was narrated from Urwah on this from Aishah of the description of the witr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became confused, there is no proof in what he narrated from her on that, and we returned to what others narrated from her."
"1589","1689 - We examined that, and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make witr with nine rakahs."
"1590","1690 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make witr with nine rakahs."
"1591","1691 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make witr with nine; when he reached old age and became heavy he made witr with seven."
"1592","1692 - Abu Ayyub - meaning Ibn Khalaf al-Tabarani - narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. In this hadith is that his witr was nine."
"1593","1693 - Except that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim. Abu Jafar said: As I think, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray at night nine rakahs. In this hadith is that those nine are his prayer which he would pray at night. This contradicts what preceded of the hadith of al-Aswad. And it is possible that all he called witr is all his prayer in which is the witr. The proof of that is what is in the hadith of Yahya ibn al-Jazzar that he would pray before weakening nine, and when he reached old age he prayed seven. That agreed with what Sad ibn Hisham narrated in his hadith of the eight which he would pray first and make witr with one; when he became bulky he made those eight six and made witr with the seventh. This indicates that he called all his night prayer in which was the witr, witr, so that these reports agree and do not contradict - except that we have not yet stood upon the reality of the witr except in the hadith of Zurarah ibn Awfa from Sad ibn Hisham in particular. We examined whether in other than that is proof of how the witr is as well."
"1594","1694 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite in the two rakahs after which he would make witr: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and: Say, O disbelievers; and recite in the one of the witr: Say, He is Allah, One, and: Say, I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak, and: Say, I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind."
"1595","1695 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make witr with three: reciting in the first rakah: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, in the second: Say, O disbelievers, and in the third: Say, He is Allah, One, and the two Muawwidhats. Amrah thus informed from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in this hadith of how the witr was, and she agreed on that with Sad ibn Hisham. And Sad added over her that he would not give salam except at the end of them."
"1596","1696 - Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Rahabi, from Abu Idris, from Abu Musa, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in his witr, in three rak'ahs: {Say: He is Allah, the One}, and the two Mu'awwidhatayn. This hadith thus also agrees with what Sa'd and Amrah narrated."
"1597","1697 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qays, who said: I said to A'ishah: With how many did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform witr? She said: He would perform witr with four and three, and eight and three, and ten and three, and he would not perform witr with less than seven nor with more than thirteen. In this hadith is her mention of what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray at night of voluntary prayer, and her calling it witr, except that she separated between the three and what she mentioned along with it, and that is only because the three had a meaning distinct from the meaning of what preceded it. That indicates the meaning of the hadith of al-Aswad, Masruq and Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from A'ishah, that it is likewise. And the proof of that also is what was narrated from her of her statement:"
"1598","1698 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr ibn Shaybah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The witr was seven and five, and the three were butayra'. She disliked making the witr three with nothing preceding them, until something other than them precedes them. Since the witr in her view was at its best when voluntary prayer preceded it, either four or two, she thereby combined the night voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by which the witr after it became sound, and the witr, and she called all of that witr. Yet it is established in the totality of that from her that the witr is three, and there is established from her narration from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Sa'd ibn Hisham narrated from her, in agreement with her own opinion. By that it is established that the witr is three, with taslim only at the end of them. As for what Hisham ibn Urwah narrated from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform witr with five, not sitting except at the end of them, we found no meaning for it. The generality of narrators narrated from his father and from others, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, contrary to that. What the generality narrated is more entitled than what he alone narrated and was isolated with. And there have been narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that reports whose meaning also returns to the meaning to which the meaning of the hadith of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, returned. Among that is what:"
"1599","1699 - Ibn Marzuq and Bakkar narrated to us, they said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray at night thirteen rak'ahs. And among that is what:"
"1600","1700 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he stayed overnight at his aunt Maymunah's. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at night praying, so I stood and performed ablution, then I stood on his left, so he pulled me and turned me to his right, and he prayed thirteen rak'ahs, his standing in them being equal. And among that is what:"
"1601","1701 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, who said: I heard Kurayb narrating from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he mentioned the like, and said: The prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) totalled thirteen rak'ahs. This hadith and the hadith of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, agree on the total of his prayer, that it was thirteen rak'ahs. Except that there is no detail in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, so we wanted to see: has anything been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in detailing that?"
"1602","1702 - So we examined that, and Ali ibn Ma'bad had narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, from his father, who said: al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded me to spend the night with the family of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and instructed me not to sleep until I memorized for him the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I prayed the Isha with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he slept, then he stood, urinated, then performed ablution, then prayed two rak'ahs, neither long nor short, then returned to his bed, then slept until I heard his snoring or heavy breathing, then he sat up and did the like of that, until he prayed six rak'ahs and performed witr with three."
"1603","1703 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas, who said: My father narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"1604","1704 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he said: Then he performed witr, and did not say with three. So Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas informed from his father about the witr of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), how it was in that prayer of his, and that it was three. And he differed with Abu Jamrah, Ikrimah ibn Khalid and Kurayb regarding the number of the voluntary prayer."
"1605","1705 - As for Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in that what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, who said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr saying, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both. Ha:"
"1606","1706 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us. (Ha) 1707 - And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I spent the night in the house of my aunt Maymunah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Isha, then came and prayed four, then stood and prayed five rak'ahs, then prayed two rak'ahs, then slept until I heard his snoring or heavy breathing, then went out to prayer. In this hadith is that he prayed eleven rak'ahs, among them two rak'ahs after the witr. It agrees with Ali ibn Abdullah regarding the nine, among which is the witr, and adds over him two rak'ahs after the witr. And there has been narrated from Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding the witr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singly, what indicates that it is three."
"1607","1708 - Among that is what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform witr with three rak'ahs."
"1608","1709 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Luwayn narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1609","1710 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Luwayn narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mukhawwal, from Muslim al-Batin, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform witr with three, reciting in the first {Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High}, and in the second {Say: O you disbelievers}, and in the third {Say: He is Allah, the One}."
"1610","1711 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Isra'il informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1611","1712 - So in this is confirmation of what Ali ibn Abdullah narrated from his father, of the witr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that it was three. As for Kurayb, he narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in that what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abi Namir narrated to us, that Kurayb informed him that he heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: I spent a night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he finished the last Isha, I left with him. When he entered the house, he bowed two light rak'ahs, their bowing like their prostration, and their prostration like their standing. Then he lay down in his place in his prayer spot, until I heard his snoring, then he woke, then performed ablution and prayed two rak'ahs likewise, then lay down a second time in his place and slept until I heard his snoring, then did the like of that five times, so he prayed ten rak'ahs, then performed witr with one, and Bilal came to him and announced the dawn, so he prayed two rak'ahs, then went out to prayer. In this hadith he informed that he prayed ten rak'ahs, then performed witr with one. It may be that he performed witr with one along with two that had preceded it, so they along with this one are three, so that the meaning of this hadith and the meaning of the hadith of Ali ibn Abdullah, Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Yahya ibn al-Jazzar become equal. Then we looked: was there narrated from him what clarifies that?"
"1612","1713 - And Ibrahim ibn Munqidh al-Usfuri had narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub, who said: Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id ibn Qays narrated to us, from Makhramah ibn Sulayman, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rak'ahs after Isha, then two, then two, then two, then performed witr with three. This hadith and the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud agree that all that he prayed was eleven rak'ahs, and this clarifies that the witr in them is three. By that it is established that the meaning of the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud, then he performed witr with one, means: along with two that had preceded it, which along with it are witr."
"1613","1714 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Makhramah ibn Sulayman, from Kurayb, that Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to him that he spent a night at Maymunah, his aunt. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rak'ahs, then two, then two, then two, then two, then two, then performed witr, then lay down, then the mu'adhdhin came to him, so he stood and prayed two light rak'ahs, then went out and prayed the dawn. In this hadith he added two rak'ahs, and did not differ with him regarding the witr. What we narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, when its meanings were gathered, indicates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform witr with three. And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, of his own statement in that something."
"1614","1715 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ata' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I dislike that it be butayra' three, but rather seven or five."
"1615","1716 - Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, and he mentioned with its isnad the like of it."
"1616","1717 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from al-A'mash, and he mentioned with its isnad the like of it. This in our view is upon his disliking to perform witr as a witr preceded by no voluntary prayer, and his loving that voluntary prayer precede it, either two rak'ahs or four. If one says: there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, contrary to this. He mentioned what:"
"1617","1718 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Ata', who said: A man said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: What do you say about Mu'awiyah, he performed witr with one, intending to disparage Mu'awiyah. Ibn Abbas said: Mu'awiyah was right. It was said to him: there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding this act of Mu'awiyah what indicates his disapproval of him for it."
"1618","1719 - That is that Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Hudayr informed us, from Ikrimah, that he said: I was with Ibn Abbas at Mu'awiyah's, conversing until a portion of the night had gone. Mu'awiyah stood and bowed one rak'ah. Ibn Abbas said: From where do you think the donkey took it?"
"1619","1720 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Imran narrated to us, and he mentioned with its isnad the like of it, except that he did not say: the donkey. It may be that the statement of Ibn Abbas, Mu'awiyah was right, was upon taqiyyah toward him, meaning: he was right in something else, because he was in his era. It is not permissible in our view that what contradicted the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which he knew, be sound. And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas regarding the witr that it is three."
"1620","1721 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Salih, from Abu Mansur, who said: I asked Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about the witr, and he said: Three. Ibn Lahi'ah said: 1722 - And Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Walid ibn Abdah, from Abu Mansur, with that."
"1621","1723 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu Yahya, who said: al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, conversed at night until the redness of dawn rose, then Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, slept and did not wake except by the voices of the people of al-Zawra'. He said to his companions: Do you think I can catch praying three, meaning the witr, and the two rak'ahs of Fajr and the dawn prayer, before the sun rises? They said: Yes, so he prayed. This was at the end of the time of Fajr, so it is impossible that the witr in his view be sufficed with less than three, then he pray it at that time as three, along with what he feared of missing Fajr. That indicates the soundness of what we directed to it the meanings of his hadiths regarding the witr, that it is three. And there has been narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib regarding the witr also that it is three."
"1622","1724 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform witr with nine surahs of the Mufassal. In the first rak'ah: {Rivalry in increase diverted you}, and {Indeed, We sent it down in the Night of Decree}, and {When it is shaken}. And in the second: {By time}, and {When the help of Allah comes}, and {Indeed, We have given you al-Kawthar}. And in the third: {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Perish}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}. And Imran ibn Husayn narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of that."
"1623","1725 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the witr, in the first rak'ah: {Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High}, and in the second: {Say: O you disbelievers}, and in the third: {Say: He is Allah, the One}."
"1624","1726 - And there was narrated from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, that Abdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah informed him, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he said: I shall surely observe the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So I pillowed his threshold or his tent. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two light rak'ahs, then prayed two long, long, long rak'ahs three times, then prayed two rak'ahs, they being less than the two before them, then prayed two rak'ahs, they being less than the two before them, then performed witr. That is thirteen rak'ahs. The discussion regarding this is like the discussion in what preceded it. And there has been narrated from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what:"
"1625","1727 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn Zadhadh narrated to us, from Abu Ghalib, from Abu Umamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform witr with nine. When he became bulky and his flesh increased, he performed witr with seven, and prayed two rak'ahs sitting, reciting in them {When it is shaken}, and {Say: O you disbelievers}. It may be that he mentioned his shaf', which is the voluntary prayer, and his witr, and made all of that witr, as we have mentioned in some of what our mention of it preceded. And we have narrated from Abu Umamah of his act what indicates this."
"1626","1728 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hayyan narrated to us, from Abu Ghalib, that Abu Umamah used to perform witr with three. By that it is established that the witr in Abu Umamah's view is what we mentioned. It is impossible that that be so in his view, while he knew contrary to it of the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Rather what he knew of the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), its meaning is what we directed it to, and Allah knows best. And there has been narrated in that from Umm al-Darda', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what:"
"1627","1729 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Umm al-Darda', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform witr with thirteen rak'ahs. When he grew old and weak, he performed witr with seven. The discussion regarding this is like the discussion regarding the hadith of Abu Umamah also. And there has been narrated in that from Umm Salamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what:"
"1628","1730 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Umm Salamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform witr with five and with seven, not separating between them with taslim nor speech. It may be that this was before the witr was fixed, so whoever wished performed witr with five, and whoever wished performed witr with seven. What was intended from them was only that they pray a witr with no known number. And there has been narrated from Abu Ayyub what indicates that it was so."
"1629","1731 - al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Husayn informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform witr with five. If you cannot, then with three. If you cannot, then with one. If you cannot, then gesture with gesturing."
"1630","1732 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ata' ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The witr is a right. Whoever performs witr with five, it is good. Whoever performs witr with three has done well. Whoever performs witr with one, it is good. Whoever cannot, let him gesture with gesturing."
"1631","1733 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn al-Dahhak narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Ata' ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyub, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The witr is a right. Whoever wishes, let him perform witr with five. Whoever wishes, let him perform witr with three. Whoever wishes, let him perform witr with one."
"1632","1734 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Ayyub, who said: The witr is a right or an obligation. Whoever wishes performs witr with seven. Whoever wishes performs witr with five. Whoever wishes performs witr with three. Whoever wishes performs witr with one. Whoever is overcome to gesturing, let him gesture. In this hadith he informed that they were given choice to perform witr with whatever they loved, with no time in that, nor number, so long as what they prayed was witr. The ummah after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreed upon contrary to that, and performed a witr none of those who perform witr in their view may leave any of it. Their consensus indicates the abrogation of what preceded it of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, would not gather them upon misguidance."
"1633","1735 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mutarrif ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Dharr, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, that he prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the witr. He recited in the first {Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High}, and in the second {Say: O you disbelievers}, and in the third {Say: He is Allah, the One}. When he finished, he said: Glory be to the King, the Holy, three times, prolonging his voice with the third."
"1634","1736 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zubayd, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1635","1737 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Zubayd, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad. Except that he said: And in the second (Say to those who disbelieve), meaning {Say: O you disbelievers}, and in the third: Allah, the One, the Eternal. This indicates that he used to perform witr with three. And there has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what:"
"1636","1738 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Do not perform witr with three. Perform witr with five or seven, and do not resemble the Maghrib prayer."
"1637","1739 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, who narrated to him, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he did not raise it, who said: Do not perform witr with three rak'ahs, resembling the Maghrib. Rather perform witr with five or seven or nine or eleven. It may be that he disliked singling out the witr until along with it be a shaf', upon what we narrated before this from Ibn Abbas and A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with them, so that be a voluntary prayer before the witr. In that is the negation of the one that it be witr. It may be upon the meaning we mentioned of the hadith of Abu Ayyub regarding choice, except that in it is not the permissibility of the witr with one. By these reports we narrated, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is established that the witr is more than one rak'ah, and nothing has been narrated regarding one rak'ah, and its interpretation bears what we explained and clarified in its place of this chapter. Then we wanted to seek that by way of reasoning, and we found the witr is not free of one of two aspects: either it is obligatory or a sunnah. If it is obligatory, we have not seen any of the obligations except upon three aspects: among them what is two rak'ahs, among them what is four, and among them what is three. Each has agreed that the witr is not two nor four. By that it is established that it is three. This is if it is obligatory. As for if it is a sunnah, we have not found any of the sunnahs except that it has a like in the obligatory. Of that is prayer: of it is voluntary, and of it is obligatory. Of that is charities: it has an origin in the obligatory, which is zakah. Of that is fasting, and it has an origin in the obligatory."
"1638","1740 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him. (Ha) 1741 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab commanded Ubayy ibn Ka'b and Tamim al-Dari to stand for the people with eleven rak'ahs. He said: The reciter would recite with the Mi'in until he would lean upon the staff from the length of standing, and we would not leave except at the branches of dawn. This indicates that they used to perform witr with three, because it is not permissible that they would pray a single shaf' then leave upon it, until they join it with another shaf'."
"1639","1742 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr informed me, from Ibn Abi Hilal, from Ibn al-Sabbaq, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: We buried Abu Bakr at night. Umar said: I have not performed witr. He stood and we lined up behind him, and he prayed with us three rak'ahs, not giving taslim except at the end of them."
"1640","1743 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaldah narrated to us, he said: I asked Abu al-Aliyah about the witr, and he said: The companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us, or they taught us, that the witr is like the Maghrib prayer, except that we recite in the third. This is the witr of the night, and this is the witr of the day."
"1641","1744 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Mihran, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: The witr is three, like the witr of the day, the Maghrib prayer."
"1642","1745 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1643","1746 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, they said: The witr is three rak'ahs, and he used to perform witr with three rak'ahs."
"1644","1747 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, he said: Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, led me in the witr prayer, I on his right and his umm walad behind us, three rak'ahs, not giving taslim except at the end of them. I thought he intended to teach me."
"1645","1748 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Nafi' and al-Maqburi, they heard Mu'adh al-Qari' giving taslim in the two rak'ahs of the witr."
"1646","1749 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from Amir ibn Yahya, from Hanash al-San'ani, who said: Mu'adh used to recite for the people in Ramadan, and he would perform witr with one, separating between it and the two with taslim, until those behind him heard his taslim. When he died, Zayd ibn Thabit stood for the people, and performed witr with three, not giving taslim until he finished them. The people said to him: Have you turned away from the sunnah of your companion? He said: No, but if I give taslim, the people disperse. These all, among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to perform witr with three. Among them were those who gave taslim in the two, and among them were those who did not give taslim. When it is established from them that the witr is three, we looked into the ruling of the taslim between the two of them, how is it? We saw the taslim cuts the prayer, and the one giving taslim exits by it from it, until he is in other than prayer. We have seen what they agreed upon of the obligatory, it is not proper that part of it be separated from part with taslim. So reasoning upon that was that it be likewise: the witr, part of it is not proper to be separated from part with taslim."
"1647","1750 - If one says: it has been narrated from more than one of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would perform witr with one, he mentioned what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman al-Khuza'i narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman al-Taymi, who said: I said: No one shall overcome me tonight in standing. I stood praying and found the sense of a man behind me at my back. I looked, and behold, Uthman ibn Affan. I moved aside for him, so he advanced and opened the Qur'an until he completed it, then bowed and prostrated. I said: Did the shaykh err? When he prayed, I said: O Commander of the Believers, you only prayed one rak'ah. He said: Yes, it is my witr. It was said to him: It may be that Uthman would separate between his shaf' and his witr, so he had prayed his shaf' before that, then performed witr at the time Abd al-Rahman saw him. In Abd al-Rahman's disapproval of Uthman's act is proof that the custom which had run before that and which he knew was upon other than what Uthman did. Abd al-Rahman has companionship. By that this meaning entered into the first meaning. If one argues in that with what was narrated from Sa'd, he had:"
"1648","1751 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, who narrated to them, from Ya'qub ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, who said: Whoever you wish of the family of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas testified before me, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas used to perform witr with one."
"1649","1752 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from his father, that he used to perform witr with one."
"1650","1753 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Salamah, who said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas led us in the last Isha prayer. When he finished, he withdrew to a side of the mosque and prayed one rak'ah. I followed him and took his hand and said to him: O Abu Ishaq, what is this rak'ah? He said: A witr, I sleep upon it. Amr said: I mentioned that to Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, and he said: He used to perform witr with one rak'ah, meaning Sa'd. It was said to him: It may be that Sa'd did in that what bears what Uthman did in what we mentioned before it. If one says: in the hadith of Amr ibn Murrah is what indicates contrary to that, because he said: He prayed with us, and when he finished, he withdrew and prayed one rak'ah. It was said to him: It may be that that finishing was the finishing to his house, and he had prayed before that, after his finishing from his prayer."
"1651","1754 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Amir, who said: The family of Sa'd and the family of Abdullah ibn Umar would give taslim in the two rak'ahs of the witr, and perform witr with one rak'ah each. Al-Sha'bi clarified in this hadith the madhhab of the family of Sa'd regarding the witr. They are those following Sa'd, following his act. Their witr which was one rak'ah each is only a witr after a prayer, between which and it they separated with taslim. That returned to the statement of those who held that the witr is three."
"1652","1755 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Mas'ud disapproved that of Sa'd. It is impossible in our view that Abdullah disapproved that of Sa'd, with the nobility of Sa'd and his knowledge, except for a meaning established with him, which is more entitled than his act. Had Ibn Mas'ud only opposed him by his opinion, his opinion would not be more entitled than Sa'd's opinion, and he would not have disapproved that of Sa'd, when what he took that from him with was opinion. Rather what Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, knew of what contradicted Sa'd's act in that is other than opinion. If one argues in that with what:"
"1653","1756 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, from Abu Ubaydullah, who said: I saw Abu al-Darda', Fadalah ibn Ubayd and Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with them, entering the mosque while the people were in the dawn prayer. They would withdraw to some of the pillars, and each of them would perform witr with one rak'ah, then enter with the people into the prayer. It was said to him: It may be that that was from them after they had prayed in their houses many even rak'ahs? What they prayed in their houses was the shaf', and what they prayed in the mosque was the witr, so that also returns to the witr being three."
"1654","1757 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz fixed the witr in Madinah by the statement of the jurists: three, with taslim only at the end of them."
"1655","1758 - Abu al-Awwam Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Nizar al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from the Seven: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, Kharijah ibn Zayd, Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah and Sulayman ibn Yasar, among other shaykhs of jurisprudence, righteousness and merit. Sometimes they differed in a thing, so he took the statement of most of them and best of them in opinion. Of what I retained from them upon this description is that the witr is three, with taslim only at the end of them. These whom we mentioned, of the jurists of Madinah and their scholars, agreed that the witr is three, with taslim only at the end of them. Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz followed them upon that. No denier besides them denied that. Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab knew what was of Sa'd's witr, yet he gave fatwa with other than it and saw it more entitled than it. Urwah ibn al-Zubayr also gave fatwa with that. Al-Zuhri and his son Hisham narrated from him regarding the witr what our narration of it preceded in this chapter. This in our view is of what ought not be opposed, for what testified to it of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the act of his companions and the statements of most of them after him, then their followers agreed upon it."
"1656","32 - Chapter of recitation in the two rak'ahs of Fajr. Abu Ja'far said: A people said: Nothing is recited in the two rak'ahs of Fajr. Others said: Only the Fatihah of the Book is recited in them. Both groups argued in that with what: 1759 - Yunus narrated to me, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Hafsah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the mu'adhdhin fell silent from the adhan for the dawn prayer or the call to dawn, would pray two light rak'ahs before the prayer was established."
"1657","1760 - Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi'. He mentioned with its isnad the like of it. They held that the sunnah in them is brevity. Among those who said that only the Fatihah of the Book is recited in them is Malik ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"1658","1761 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik said: With that I take for myself specially, that I recite in them the Umm al-Qur'an."
"1659","1762 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr as two light rak'ahs, until I would say: Did he recite in them the Umm al-Kitab?"
"1660","1763 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, and he mentioned with its isnad the like of it."
"1661","1764 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, that Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to him, that Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to him, from his mother Amrah, that A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"1662","1765 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: I heard my aunt Amrah narrating from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when dawn rose, would pray two light rak'ahs. I say: he recites in them the Fatihah of the Book. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith of Shu'bah is contrary to what is in the other hadiths of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, before it, because he said: She said: I say: He recited in them the Fatihah of the Book. In this is affirmation of his recitation in them. That is a proof against whoever denied recitation from them. It may be that he recites in them the Fatihah of the Book and other than it, and he lightens the recitation greatly, until she says in astonishment at his brevity: Did he recite in them the Fatihah of the Book? There has been narrated from her, disconnected, what is in it that he used to recite in them other than the Fatihah of the Book."
"1663","1766 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad, that A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would conceal what he recited in them. She mentioned {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}. It is established from him by the hadith of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, which Shu'bah narrated, the recitation of the Fatihah of the Book, and by this hadith of Abu Bakrah, the recitation of {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}. By that it is established that he would do in them what he does in the rest of prayers of recitation. Then we looked: did other than A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrate in that anything?"
"1664","1767 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn al-Walid ibn Ma'dan narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah, who said: I cannot count what I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the two rak'ahs before Fajr and the two rak'ahs after Maghrib: {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}."
"1665","1768 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Isra'il informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Mujahid. (Ha) 1769 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, who said: I observed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twenty-four times or twenty-five times reciting in the two rak'ahs before the dawn prayer and in the two rak'ahs after Maghrib: {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}."
"1666","1770 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (Ha) 1771 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, they said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Hakim al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Yasar informed us that he heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr, in the first of them {Say: We believe in Allah and what was sent down to us}, the verse. And in the second {Say: We believe in Allah} {and bear witness that we are Muslims}."
"1667","1772 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu al-Ghayth saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the two prostrations before Fajr, in the first prostration {Say: We believe in Allah and what was sent down to us and what was sent down to Ibrahim}, the verse. And in the second prostration {Our Lord, we believe in what You sent down and we followed the Messenger, so write us with the witnesses}."
"1668","1773 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Musa ibn Khalaf al-Ammi narrated to us, he said: My brother Khalaf ibn Musa narrated to us, from his father, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}."
"1669","1774 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Unays al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: I heard Talhah ibn Khirash narrating from Jabir that a man stood and bowed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. He recited in the first {Say: O you disbelievers}, until the surah ended. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is a servant who believed in his Lord. Then he stood and recited in the other {Say: He is Allah, the One}, until the surah ended. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is a servant who knew his Lord. Talhah said: I like to recite these two surahs in these two rak'ahs. In these reports, in some of them is that he recited {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}, and in some of them is that he recited other than that. There is not in that a negation that he recited the Fatihah of the Book along with what he recited of that. By what we described it is established that his brevity of that was a brevity along with recitation. By what we mentioned of his recitation of other than the Fatihah of the Book is established the negation of the statement of whoever disliked that other than the Fatihah of the Book be recited in them. It is established that they are like the rest of voluntary prayer, and that there is recited in them as is recited in voluntary prayer. We have not found any of the voluntary prayers in which nothing is recited, and in which only the Fatihah of the Book is recited. We have not found any of the voluntary in which prolonging the recitation was disliked. Rather length of qunut has been liked, and that was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Of that is what:"
"1670","1775 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Mihran narrated to us. (Ha) 1776 - And Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Which prayer is most excellent? He said: Length of qunut."
"1671","1777 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu al-Zubayr narrating from Jabir that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most excellent prayer is length of standing."
"1672","1778 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most excellent prayer is length of standing."
"1673","1779 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Ali al-Azdi, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Abdullah ibn Habashi al-Khath'ami, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which prayers are most excellent? He said: Length of standing."
"1674","1780 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us, he said: Suwayd Abu Hatim narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr al-Laythi narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Which prayer is most excellent? He said: Length of qunut."
"1675","1781 - I heard Ibn Abi Imran saying: I heard Ibn Sama'ah saying: I heard Muhammad ibn al-Hasan saying: With that we take. It is more excellent in our view than much bowing and prostration with little length of standing. Since this is the ruling of voluntary prayer, and the two rak'ahs of Fajr were made of the noblest of voluntary prayer, and their matter was emphasized what was not emphasized of the matter of other than them of voluntary prayer. There was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding them what:"
"1676","1782 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Qunfudh, from Ibn Silan, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not leave the two rak'ahs of Fajr, even if the horses chase you."
"1677","1783 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata' narrated to me, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not upon any of the supererogatory acts more strictly observant than upon the two rak'ahs before Fajr."
"1678","1784 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata', and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1679","1785 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Sa'd ibn Hisham, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two rak'ahs of Fajr are better than the world and what is in it. Abu Ja'far said: Since they were the noblest of voluntary prayer, it was more entitled that there be done in them the noblest of what is done in voluntary prayer."
"1680","1786 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuja' narrated to me, from al-Hasan ibn Ziyad, who said: I heard Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, saying: Perhaps I recited in the two rak'ahs of Fajr my hizb of the Qur'an. With this we take: there is no harm in prolonging the recitation in them? It is in our view more excellent than shortening, because that is of the length of qunut which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preferred in voluntary prayer over other than it. There has been narrated in that also from Ibrahim."
"1681","1787 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us. (Ha) 1788 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ibrahim, who said: When dawn rises, there is no prayer except the two rak'ahs before Fajr. I said to Ibrahim: Shall I prolong the recitation in them? He said: Yes, if you wish. There have been narrated reports from those after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding recitation in them. I intended by mentioning them the proof against whoever said: there is no recitation in them. Of that is what:"
"1682","1789 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, who said: Ibn Mas'ud used to recite in the two rak'ahs after Maghrib and in the two rak'ahs before dawn: {Say: O you disbelievers}, and {Say: He is Allah, the One}."
"1683","1790 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from his companions, that they used to do that."
"1684","1791 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash informed me, from Ibrahim, that the companions of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to do that."
"1685","1792 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Ala' ibn al-Musayyab, that Abu Wa'il recited in the two rak'ahs of Fajr the Fatihah of the Book and a verse."
"1686","1793 - Yunus and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated to me, from Uqbah ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, that he heard Abdullah ibn Amr reciting in the two rak'ahs of Fajr the Umm al-Qur'an, not adding anything with it."
"1687","33 - Chapter of the two rak'ahs after Asr. 1794 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad and Masruq, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: There was no day in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with me except that he prayed two rak'ahs after Asr."
"1688","1795 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Shaybani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from his father, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: Two rak'ahs the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not leave, secretly nor openly: two rak'ahs before dawn, and two rak'ahs after Asr."
"1689","1796 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, then he mentioned with its isnad the like of it."
"1690","1797 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, from Masruq, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not leave the two rak'ahs after Asr."
"1691","1798 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Abbad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never left the two rak'ahs with me after Asr ever."
"1692","1799 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never entered upon me in my house after Asr except that he prayed two rak'ahs."
"1693","1800 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Rijal narrated to us, from his father, from Amrah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"1694","1801 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Umm Musa, who said: I came to A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and asked her about the two rak'ahs after Asr, and she mentioned from her the like of that also."
"1695","1802 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, from his father, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray the Asr prayer, then pray after it two rak'ahs."
"1696","1803 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Sa'd al-A'ma narrating from a man called al-Sa'ib, freedman of the Persians, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he saw him bow two rak'ahs after Asr and said: I shall not leave them after I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying them. Abu Ja'far said: A people went to this and said: There is no harm in a man praying after Asr two rak'ahs, and they are of the sunnah in their view. They argued in that with this hadith. Most of the scholars opposed them in that and disliked them. They argued in that with what:"
"1697","1804 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Yahya informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, that Mu'awiyah sent to Umm Salamah asking her about the two rak'ahs which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed after Asr. She said: Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with me two rak'ahs after Asr. I said: Were you commanded with them? He said: No, but I used to pray them after Zuhr, and I was distracted from them, so I prayed them now."
"1698","1805 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Labid, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan said, while upon the minbar, to Kathir ibn al-Salt: Go to A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and ask her about the two rak'ahs of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Asr. Abu Salamah said: So I stood with him. Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said to Abdullah ibn al-Harith: Go with him. We came to her and asked her. She said: I do not know; ask Umm Salamah. We asked her, and she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me one day after Asr and prayed two rak'ahs. I said: O Messenger of Allah, you did not use to pray these two rak'ahs? He said: A delegation of Banu Tamim came to me, or charity came to me, and they distracted me from two rak'ahs I used to pray after Zuhr, and they are these two."
"1699","1806 - al-Hajjaj ibn Imran ibn al-Fadl al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata' narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sufyan, that Mu'awiyah sent to A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, asking her about the two prostrations after Asr. She said: He did not pray them with me, but Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me that he prayed them with her. He sent to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed them with me. I had not seen him pray them before nor after. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what are two prostrations I saw you pray after Asr, you had not prayed them before nor after? He said: They are two prostrations I used to pray after Zuhr. Qala'is of charity came to me, so I forgot them until I prayed Asr. Then I remembered them, and I disliked to pray them in the mosque while the people saw me, so I prayed them with you."
"1700","1807 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Dhakwan, from A'ishah, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in her house two rak'ahs after Asr. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what are these two rak'ahs? He said: I used to pray them after Zuhr, then wealth came to me and distracted me, so I prayed them now."
"1701","1808 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, that Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to him that Ibn Abbas, Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah sent him to A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her. They said: Convey salam to her from us all, and ask her about the two rak'ahs after Asr. Say: We have been informed that you pray them, and it has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them. Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: I used to strike the people along with Umar upon them. Kurayb said: I entered upon her and conveyed to her what they sent me with. She said: Ask Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her. I went out to them and informed them of her statement. They returned me to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, with the like of what they sent me with to A'ishah. Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding them, then I saw him pray them. As for when he prayed them, he prayed Asr, then entered upon me, and with me were women of Banu Haram of the Ansar. He prayed them, so I sent to him the slave girl and said: Stand at his side and say: Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, says to you: O Messenger of Allah, why do I hear you forbidding these two rak'ahs while I see you praying them? If he gestures with his hand, withdraw from him. The slave girl did so, and he gestured with his hand, so she withdrew from him. When he finished, he said: O daughter of Abu Umayyah, you asked about the two rak'ahs after Asr. Some people of Abd al-Qays came to me with Islam from a people, and they distracted me from the two rak'ahs after Zuhr. They are these two. In these:"
"1702","1809 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, who said: Hizam ibn Darraj informed me that Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, glorified after Asr two rak'ahs, on the road of Makkah. Umar called him and rebuked him and said: By Allah, you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid us them."
"1703","1810 - Abdullah ibn Mu'awiyah ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Attabi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Trustworthy men testified before me, and most pleasing of them to me is Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after Fajr until the sun rises, and after Asr until the sun sets."
"1704","1811 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: More than one of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it."
"1705","1812 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with its isnad the like of it."
"1706","1813 - Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. (Ha) 1814 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray at the end of every prayer two rak'ahs, except Fajr and Asr."
"1707","1815 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after dawn until the sun rises, and prayer after Asr until the sun sets."
"1708","1816 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Dinar narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Aws narrated to us, he said: Misda' Abu Yahya narrated to me, he said: A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me, with a curtain between me and her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray a prayer except that he followed it with two rak'ahs, other than Asr and dawn, for he would make the two rak'ahs before them."
"1709","1817 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd, from Nasr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Mu'adh ibn Afra', that he circumambulated after Asr or after the dawn prayer and did not pray. He was asked about that, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after dawn until the sun rises, and prayer after Asr until the sun sets."
"1710","1818 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated to us, from Atiyyah al-Awfi, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade that as Mu'adh ibn Afra' mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1711","1819 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1712","1820 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1713","1821 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1714","1822 - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Muhammad, who said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1715","1823 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah al-Duba'i, who said: Humran ibn Aban narrated to us, who said: Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan addressed us and said: O people, you pray a prayer which we accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we did not see him praying it, and he forbade it, meaning the two rak'ahs after Asr."
"1716","1824 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after dawn until the sun rises, and after Asr until the sun sets. The reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came successive with the prohibition of prayer after Asr until the sun sets, and his companions after him acted with that. It is not proper for anyone to oppose that."
"1717","1825 - Of what was narrated from his companions in that is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, that he saw Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, striking al-Munkadir in prayer after Asr."
"1718","1826 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the like of it with its isnad."
"1719","1827 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah, who said: Umar used to dislike prayer after Asr, and I dislike what Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, disliked."
"1720","1828 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, and he mentioned with its isnad the like of it."
"1721","1829 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, who said: I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, striking the man when he saw him praying after Asr, until he left his prayer."
"1722","1830 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, who said: I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about prayer after Asr. He said: I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, striking the man when he saw him praying after Asr."
"1723","1831 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Iyad ibn Laqit narrated to us, from Iyad ibn Laqit, from al-Bara' ibn Azib, who said: Salman ibn Rabi'ah sent me as a courier to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, for a need of his. I came to him, and he said to me: Do not pray after Asr, for I fear upon you that you leave them to other than them."
"1724","1832 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Ibrahim informed me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij narrating from his father, who said: Two rak'ahs of Asr escaped me, so I stood to make them up. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to me with the dirrah. When I gave taslim, he said: What is this prayer? I said: Two rak'ahs escaped me, so I stood to make them up. He said: I thought you were praying after Asr. Had you done that, I would have done with you and done."
"1725","1833 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd, from Ubaydullah ibn Rafi', from his father, and he mentioned the like of it."
"1726","1834 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, and from Umar ibn Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah ibn Nawfal, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab commanded me to strike with the dirrah whoever would pray after Asr the two rak'ahs."
"1727","1835 - al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Mas'ud ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah, from Muhammad ibn Shaddad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from al-Ashtar, who said: Khalid ibn al-Walid would strike the people upon prayer after Asr."
"1728","1836 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amir ibn Mus'ab informed me, from Tawus, that he asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about the two rak'ahs after Asr, and he forbade him and said: {It is not for a believing man nor a believing woman, when Allah and His Messenger have decreed a matter, that they have choice in their matter}, the verse. These companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid them. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, would strike upon them in the presence of the rest of his companions, upon nearness of their covenant with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), none of them disapproving that of him. If one says: Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had forbidden them, then prayed them after that when he left them after Zuhr. So likewise I say: whoever leaves them after Zuhr prays them after Asr, and no one prays after Asr any of the voluntary other than them. It was said to him: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed them then, he forbade anyone to make them up."
"1729","1837 - That is that Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Dhakwan, from Umm Salamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Asr, then entered my house and prayed two rak'ahs. I said: O Messenger of Allah, you prayed a prayer you did not use to pray. He said: Wealth came to me and distracted me from two rak'ahs I used to pray after Zuhr, so I prayed them now. I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we make them up when they escape? He said: No. In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade anyone to pray them after Asr as make-up for what he would pray after Zuhr. That indicates that the ruling of other than him regarding them, when they escape him, is contrary to his ruling. No one may pray them after Asr, nor perform voluntary after Asr at all. This is reasoning also. That is because the two rak'ahs after Zuhr are not obligatory. When they are left until he prays the Asr prayer, if they are prayed after that, their performer only volunteered with them in other than a time of voluntary. For that we forbade anyone to pray after Asr voluntary, and made these two rak'ahs and other than them of the rest of voluntary in that equal. This is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"1730","34 - Chapter of a man praying with two men, where does he place them? Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in the chapter of tatbiq in bowing, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that he prayed with Alqamah and al-Aswad, and placed one of them on his right and the other on his left. He said: Then we bowed and placed our hands upon our knees. He struck our hands with his hand and clasped. When he finished, he said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. That bears in our view that what he mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he did is the tatbiq. It bears that it is the tatbiq and placing one of the followers on his right and the other on his left. We wanted to see: is there in any of the narrations what indicates anything of that? 1838 - And Husayn ibn Nasr had narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, who said: I entered, I and my uncle, upon Abdullah at midday. He established the prayer and we stayed behind him. He took one of us by his right and the other by his left, and placed us on his right and on his left. When he prayed, he said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would do when they were three. This hadith informs that the statement of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did, is upon the standing of the two men, one of them on his right and the other on his left, and upon the tatbiq."
"1731","1839 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, who said: I was, I and Shu'ayb ibn al-Habhab, with Ibrahim. Asr came, and Ibrahim led us in prayer. We stood behind him, so he pulled us and placed us on his right and on his left. He said: When we prayed and went out to the house, Ibrahim said: Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Thus, so pray, and do not pray as so-and-so prays. He said: I mentioned that to Muhammad ibn Sirin, and did not name Ibrahim to him. He said: This is Ibrahim. He said that from Alqamah. I do not see Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, did it except for narrowness in the mosque, or for an excuse he saw in it, not upon that being of the sunnah. He said: I mentioned it to al-Sha'bi, and he said: Alqamah ibn Awn, the speaker, claimed that. In this hadith is ascription of the act to Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him. Neither al-Sha'bi nor Ibn Sirin mentions it, from Alqamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It may also be that Alqamah did not mention that to al-Sha'bi and to Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then al-Aswad mentioned it to his son, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). However the meaning was in this? It was opposed by what:"
"1732","1840 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Abu Hazrah al-Madini Ya'qub ibn Mujahid, from Ubadah ibn al-Walid ibn Ubadah ibn al-Samit, who said: We came to Jabir ibn Abdullah. Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, until I stood on his left. He took me by my hand and turned me until he placed me on his right. Jabbar ibn Sakhr came and stood on his left. He pushed us both with his hand until we stood behind him."
"1733","1841 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that his grandmother Mulaykah invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to food she made. He ate of it, then said: Stand, so I pray for you. Anas said: I stood to a mat of ours blackened from long use, and sprinkled it with water. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, and I and the orphan lined up behind him, and the old woman behind us. He prayed with us two rak'ahs, then left. If one says: The act of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, this which we described, after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), indicates that what he acted with of that is the abrogating. It was said to him: There has been narrated from other than Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he did after the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that the like of what Jabir and Anas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated. If what was narrated from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, of his act after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is a proof with you that that is the abrogating, what was narrated from other than Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, of that, was a proof with your opponent that that is the abrogating. Of what was narrated from other than Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, in that is what:"
"1734","1842 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri. (Ha) 1843 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from his father, who said: I came at midday to Umar and found him praying. I stood on his left, so he moved me behind and placed me on his right. Then Yarfa' came, so I moved back, and I and he prayed behind him."
"1735","1844 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the family of Talhah, narrated to us, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar saying: I heard Ibn Utbah saying: The prayer was established, and in the mosque was no one except the mu'adhdhin, a man, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, placed them both behind him and prayed with them both. Then we sought its ruling by way of reasoning. We saw the origin that the imam, when he prays with one man, places him on his right. With that came the sunnah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him. And in what:"
"1736","1845 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying. I stood on his left, so he moved me behind and placed me on his right. This is the station of the one with the imam. When he prays with three, he places them behind him. There is no disagreement in this among the scholars. Their disagreement is only regarding the two. Some said: He places them where he places the one. Some said: He places them where he places the three. We wanted to look into that to know: is the ruling of the two in that like the ruling of the three? Or like the ruling of the one?"
"1737","1846 - We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two and what is above them are a congregation. Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us with that, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi and Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, they said: al-Rabi' ibn Badr narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made them both a congregation. Their ruling became like the ruling of what is more than them, not the ruling of what is less than them. We saw Allah, Mighty and Majestic, obligated for the brother or the sister from the mother the sixth, and obligated for the group the third. Likewise He obligated for the two, and made for the sister from the father the half, and for the two the two-thirds. Likewise they agreed that it is the third. They agreed that for the daughter is the half, and for the daughters the two-thirds. Most of them said, and Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, is among them: the two daughters also have the two-thirds. Likewise it is in reasoning, because the daughter, when she was in her inheritance from her father like the sister in her inheritance from her brother, the two daughters also were in their inheritance from their father like the two sisters in their inheritance from their brother. The ruling of the two in what we described was the ruling of the congregation, not the ruling of the one. Reasoning upon that is that they be in their station with the imam in prayer the station of the congregation, not the station of the one. By that is established what Jabir and Anas narrated, and what Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them, did. It is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. Except that Abu Yusuf said: The imam has choice. If he wishes, he does as Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated. If he wishes, he does as Anas and Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated. The statement of:"
"1738","35 - Chapter of the fear prayer, how is it? 1847 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali and Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, they said: Abu Awanah narrated to us. (Ha) 1848 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Darir narrated to us. (Ha) 1849 - And Abd al-Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us. 1850 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn al-Akhnas, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, obligated upon the tongue of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) four in residence, two in travel, and one in fear. Abu Ja'far said: A people went to this hadith and followed it, and made it an origin. They made the fear prayer one rak'ah. Of the proof against them in that is that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: {When you are among them and establish for them the prayer, let a group of them stand with you and take their arms. When they prostrate, let them be behind you. Let another group who have not prayed come and pray with you}. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, obligated the fear prayer, and the text of its obligation in His Book is thus. He made the prayer of the group after completion of the first rak'ah with the imam. By this it is established that the imam prays it in the state of fear two rak'ahs. This is contrary to this hadith. It is not permissible to take a hadith repelled by the text of the Book. Then there opposed it from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, other than it."
"1739","1851 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Jahm, who said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed at Dhi Qarad the fear prayer. The polytheists were between him and the qiblah. He lined a row behind him and a row facing the enemy. He prayed with them one rak'ah, then these went to the stands of these, and these returned to the stands of these. He prayed with them one rak'ah, then gave taslim upon them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had two rak'ahs, and each group one rak'ah. Abu Ja'far said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, what contradicted what Mujahid narrated from him. It is impossible that the obligation upon the imam be one rak'ah, then he pray it with another, without sitting for tashahhud nor taslim. When the two reports from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, contradicted, they negated each other. No one may argue in that with Mujahid from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, because his opponent argues against him with Ubaydullah from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, with contrary to that."
"1740","1852 - If they say: there has been narrated from other than Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, what agrees with what we said, they mentioned what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabi', from al-Qasim ibn Hassan, who said: I came to Ibn Wadi'ah and asked him about the fear prayer. He said: Go to Zayd ibn Thabit and ask him. I met him and asked him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer in some of his days. He lined a row behind him and a row facing the enemy. He prayed with them one rak'ah, then these went to the stands of these, and these came to the stands of these. He prayed with them one rak'ah, then gave taslim upon them."
"1741","1853 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with his chain. And he said Abdullah ibn Wadi'ah, and he added: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rak'ahs, and each group prayed one rak'ah each."
"1742","1854 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us. (H). 1855 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, from Tha'labah ibn Zahdam al-Hanzali, he said: We were with Sa'id ibn al-As in Tabaristan, and he said: Which of you witnessed the fear prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So Hudhayfah stood up and said: I did, then he did the same as what Zayd mentioned, exactly."
"1743","1856 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, he said: Atiyyah ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Mukhmil ibn Dimath narrated to me, he said: I campaigned with Sa'id ibn al-As, and he asked the people who among you witnessed the fear prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"1744","1857 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Yazid al-Faqir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) facing the enemy. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"1745","1858 - Abu Hazim Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, he said: Abu Hafs al-Fallas narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer with his Companions, and he mentioned the like of it. It was said to them: This does not agree with what Mujahid reported, but it agrees with what Ubaydullah reported from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him. And our proof has already been given at the beginning of this chapter; for it is impossible that the obligation upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that prayer was one rak'ah and then he joins it to another without saying salam between them. So it is established by what we mentioned that the obligation of the fear prayer upon the imam is two rak'ahs, then no making up by the followers nor anything else was mentioned in these reports. So it is possible that they made up, and by necessary reasoning they must have made up one rak'ah each; for we see the obligation upon the imam in the prayer of safety and residence is the same as upon the follower, and likewise the obligation upon both in the prayer of safety on a journey is the same, and it is impossible that the follower's obligation be one rak'ah and he joins one whose obligation is two rak'ahs except that what is due upon his imam becomes due upon him; do you not see that if a traveler joins the prayer of a resident he prays four, so what is due upon his imam becomes due upon the follower, and his obligation increases with the increase of his imam's obligation, and the follower may owe what his imam does not owe. Of that is that we see the resident pray behind the traveler, so he prays with his prayer, then he stands after that and completes the resident's prayer, so the follower may owe what his imam does not owe, while his imam does not owe what he does not owe. So when the two rak'ahs upon the imam were established by what we mentioned, it is established that the like of them is upon the follower. And there has been reported from Hudhayfah, as his own statement, what indicates what we interpreted in his hadith and the hadith of Zayd and Jabir and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that they made up one rak'ah each."
"1746","1859 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulaym ibn Id, from Hudhayfah, he said: The fear prayer is two rak'ahs and four prostrations. Abu Ja'far said: So that indicated that they had done so with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the first hadiths. Then we examined the reports, whether we find anything of that in them."
"1747","1860 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Musa, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer with his Companions, so he prayed one rak'ah with a group of them, while a group was facing the enemy, and when he had prayed one rak'ah with them he said salam, so they stepped back on their heels until they reached their brothers, then the others came and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one rak'ah with them then said salam, so each party stood up and prayed one rak'ah each. So this hadith has informed that they made up, and it clarifies what we described as possible in the first reports, and his saying: then he said salam after the first rak'ah, may be a salam by which he does not intend ending the prayer, but intends thereby informing the followers of the place of withdrawal."
"1748","1861 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, Sufyan said: (H). 1862 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer on one of his days, so he lined up a row behind him, and a row facing the enemy, all of them in prayer, so he prayed one rak'ah with them, then these went to the positions of these, and these came to the positions of these, so he prayed one rak'ah with them, then they made up one rak'ah each, then these went to the positions of these, and these came to the positions of these, so they made up one rak'ah."
"1749","1863 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Bakkar al-Qaysi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: Khusayf narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer in the Harrah of Banu Sulaym, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention: and all of them in prayer. And he added: And they were in other than the qiblah direction. Abu Ja'far said: So this hadith has informed that they made up one rak'ah each, and informed that they entered the prayer together. So it is established by the reports we mentioned that the fear prayer is two rak'ahs, except that the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, mentions their entering the prayer together. So we wanted to see whether any other hadith opposed this hadith in this meaning. So we examined that."
"1750","1864 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', that Abdullah ibn Umar, when asked about the fear prayer, would say: The imam advances with a group of people and prays one rak'ah with them, while a group of them are between him and the enemy and have not prayed, then those who have not prayed advance and the others step back, so he prays one rak'ah with them, then the imam leaves, having prayed two rak'ahs, then each of the two groups stands and prays for themselves one rak'ah each after the imam leaves, so each of the two groups will have prayed two rak'ahs each. Nafi' said: I do not think Ibn Umar mentioned that except from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this hadith has informed that the entry of the second group into the prayer is after the imam prays one rak'ah with the first group. And the Book testifies to this, for Allah the Exalted said: And let another group come who have not prayed and pray with you. So it is established by what we described that the entry of the second group into the prayer is after the imam finishes the first rak'ah. And this report is sound in chain and its origin is raised to the Prophet, even though Nafi' doubted in it at the time Malik narrated it from him. And thus his major companions reported it from him."
"1751","1865 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer on one of his days, so a group of them stood with him and a group of them between him and the enemy, so he prayed one rak'ah with them, then these went to the positions of these, and these came to the positions of these, so he prayed one rak'ah with them, then he said salam to them, then the two groups made up one rak'ah each."
"1752","1866 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Khayyat narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of its meaning. And Salim also reported it from his father, raised to the Prophet."
"1753","1867 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that he prayed it with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise."
"1754","1868 - Abu Muhammad Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim informed me that Umar said: I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his expedition toward Najd, and we faced the enemy. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"1755","1869 - And others went in that to what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Yazid ibn Ruman, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from one who prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Dhat al-Riqa' the fear prayer, that a group lined up with him and a group facing the enemy, so he prayed one rak'ah with those with him, then he remained standing and they completed for themselves, then they left and lined up facing the enemy, and the other group came, so he prayed with them the rak'ah remaining of his prayer, then he remained sitting, and they completed for themselves, then he said salam with them."
"1756","1870 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, from Salih ibn Khawwat al-Ansari, that Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah informed him that the fear prayer, and he mentioned the like of it, and he did not attribute it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he added in mentioning the last one, he said: So he bows with them and prostrates then says salam, so they stand and bow for themselves the remaining rak'ah then say salam."
"1757","1871 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id. And he mentioned the like of it with his chain. It was said to them: This hadith contains that they prayed while being followers before the imam finished the prayer, in the hadith of Yazid ibn Ruman from Salih ibn Khawwat. 1872 - And we have reported from the hadith of Shu'bah from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Salih ibn Khawwat, the opposite of that; for in the hadith of Yazid ibn Ruman he remained standing after he prayed the first rak'ah, and they completed for themselves, then they left, then the other came after that. And in the hadith of Shu'bah from Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Salih ibn Khawwat, that he prayed one rak'ah with a group of them, then these went to the positions of these, and he did not mention that they prayed before they withdrew. So al-Qasim contradicted Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Ruman. So if this is taken by the path of the chain, then Abd al-Rahman from his father al-Qasim, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is better than Yazid ibn Ruman from Salih from one who informed him, and if they are equal they contradict each other, and when they contradict, neither disputant has proof in either against the other, since his opponent has against him the like of what he has against his opponent. So if one says: Yahya ibn Sa'id reported from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl, what agrees with what Yazid ibn Ruman reported, and Yahya ibn Sa'id is not less than Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim in precision and memory. It is said to him: Yahya ibn Sa'id is as you said, but he did not raise the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), rather he stopped it at Sahl, so it is possible that what Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim reported from Salih is what was with Sahl from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) specifically, then he said the rest as his own opinion, so that became an opinion from him, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and for that Yahya did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So when that bore what we mentioned, it no longer stands as proof. And reasoning rejects that; for we have not found in any prayer that the follower prays any of it before the imam, rather the follower does it with the imam's act or after the imam's act, and knowledge of what is disputed is sought from what is agreed upon. So if they say: We have seen turning away from the qiblah may be allowed in this prayer, though not allowed in others, so why do you deny the follower's making up before the imam finishes, likewise permitted in this prayer and not permitted in others. It is said to him: Turning away from the qiblah we have seen permitted in other than this prayer for an excuse, so it was permitted in this prayer as it was permitted in others, and that is that they agreed that one who is fleeing, when prayer comes, prays even if not toward the qiblah. So when he could pray the whole prayer to other than the qiblah due to the enemy, without spoiling his prayer, his turning to other than the qiblah in part of his prayer is more likely not to harm him. So when we found an agreed principle for prayer toward other than the qiblah as permissible with excuse, we extended to it what was disputed of turning the back to the qiblah in withdrawal for an excuse, and when we found no agreed principle for the follower's making up before the imam finishes the prayer to extend it to, we nullified acting by it and returned to the other reports we mentioned first, which have mass transmission and the evidences of consensus. And there has been reported from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the opposite of all that."
"1758","1873 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah and Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Asadi informed us, that he heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from Marwan ibn al-Hakam that he asked Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: Did you pray the fear prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. Marwan said: When? Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: In the year of the Najd expedition. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for the Asr prayer and a group stood with him, and another group facing the enemy with their backs to the qiblah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir and they all said takbir with him, including those facing the enemy, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed one rak'ah and the group next to him bowed with him, then he prostrated and the group next to him prostrated with him, while the others stood facing the enemy, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and the group with him stood, so they went to the enemy and faced them, and the group that had been facing the enemy came forward, so they bowed and prostrated, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained standing as he was, then they stood, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed another rak'ah and they bowed with him, then he prostrated and they prostrated with him, then the other group that had been facing the enemy came forward, so they bowed and prostrated, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting with those with him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said salam and they all said salam with him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had two rak'ahs, and each man of the two groups had two rak'ahs each."
"1759","1874 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer, so he split the people into two halves, so a group prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and a group toward the enemy, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one rak'ah with those behind him and prostrated two prostrations with them, then he stood and they stood with him, and when they stood upright, those behind him moved back behind them, stepping back, so they stood behind those facing the enemy. And the others came and stood behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so they prayed for themselves one rak'ah, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was standing, then they stood, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed another with them, so they and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had two rak'ahs. And those facing the enemy came and prayed for themselves one rak'ah and two prostrations, then they sat behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said salam with them all. So in this hadith is the imam turning toward the enemy, with the group that prayed the rak'ah with him, and that is in none of the reports except this hadith. And in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is what indicates rejection of that; for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: Let a group of them stand with you and take their arms, and when they prostrate let them be behind you, and let another group come who have not prayed and pray with you. So in this verse are two meanings requiring rejection of this hadith, one of them: His saying: Who have not prayed, let them pray with you, so this indicates that their entry into the prayer is only at the time of their coming, not before that, and His saying: Let a group of them stand with you. Then He said: And let another group come who have not prayed and pray with you, so He mentioned the coming of both groups to the imam. And that is agreed with by the mass-transmitted reports from the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we began by mentioning, so they are more worthy than this hadith."
"1760","1875 - And others went in the fear prayer to what Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from al-Ash'ath, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer with them, so he prayed two rak'ahs with a group of them, then they left, and the others came, so he prayed two rak'ahs with them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed four, and each group prayed two rak'ahs."
"1761","1876 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1762","1877 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhat al-Riqa', and the prayer was called. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"1763","1878 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was fighting Khasafah, so he prayed the fear prayer with them, and he mentioned the like of that also. So a people held by this, and claimed the fear prayer is so. And they have no proof with us in these reports, for it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it so because he was not on a journey in which prayer is shortened, so he prayed two rak'ahs with each group, then they made up after that two rak'ahs each. And thus we say if the enemy comes in a city and its people want to pray the fear prayer, they do so. That is after that prayer is Zuhr or Asr or Isha. They said: The making up was not mentioned. It was said to them: It is possible they made up and that was not conveyed in the report, and much like this comes in reports, and if they did not make up, then that with us is also no proof; for it is possible that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the obligation was prayed at that time twice, so each of them was an obligation, and that was done at the beginning of Islam then abrogated."
"1764","1879 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim informed us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Sulayman, the freedman of Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, he said: I came to the mosque and saw Ibn Umar sitting while the people were in prayer, so I said: Will you not pray with the people? He said: I have prayed in my lodging; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that an obligatory prayer be prayed twice. So prohibition is only after permission. So the Muslims used to do so at the start of Islam, praying in their homes then coming to the mosque and praying that prayer they reached as an obligation, so they would have prayed an obligation twice in a day, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them that, and ordered after that whoever comes to the mosque and reaches that prayer to pray it and make it supererogatory. And Ibn Umar's leaving prayer with the people bears with us two interpretations. It may be that prayer was one after which no supererogatory is allowed, so it was not allowed to pray it except as an obligation, so he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade praying an obligation twice in a day, that is so it is not allowed to pray it as an obligation, for I have prayed it once, and I do not join them, for supererogatory is not allowed for me at that time. And it may be that he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prohibition of repeating it in this sense he forbade, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted after that to pray it as supererogatory, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, did not hear that."
"1765","1880 - So we examined that, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Majishun narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Rafi', he said: Muhriz ibn Abi Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, sent me to Ibn Umar to ask him: If a man prays Zuhr in his house then comes to the mosque while the people pray and prays with them, which of them is his prayer? So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: His first prayer. So in this hadith Ibn Umar held the second to be supererogatory, so that indicated his leaving prayer in the hadith of Sulayman was only because it was a prayer after which no supererogatory is allowed. So if it was in the hadith of Abu Bakrah and Jabir which we mentioned, the worthiest ruling is what we described, that one who prayed an obligation, it was allowed to repeat it and it be an obligation, so for that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it twice with the two groups, and that was allowed had the ruling remained so. But when it was abrogated and he forbade praying an obligation twice, that meaning for which he prayed two rak'ahs with each group was lifted and acting by it ended. So they have no proof in the hadith of Abu Bakrah and Jabir for their bearing what we mentioned."
"1766","1881 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Hibban, meaning Ibn Hilal, narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Khalid ibn Ayman al-Ma'afiri, he said: The people of al-Awali used to pray in their homes, and pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them to repeat prayer twice in a day. Amr said: I mentioned that to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and he said: He spoke the truth. And there has been reported from Jabir ibn Abdillah in this what indicates other than this meaning."
"1767","1882 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Sulayman al-Yashkuri, that he asked Jabir ibn Abdillah about shortening prayer in fear, on which day it was revealed and where is it? He said: We went out to meet a caravan of Quraysh coming from Sham, until when we were at Nakhl, a man of the people came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Are you Muhammad? He said: Yes. He said: Do you not fear me? He said: No. He said: Who prevents you from me? He said: Allah prevents me from you. He said: So he drew the sword, he said: So the people threatened him and warned him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for departure and they took arms, then prayer was called, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with a group of the people, while another group guarded them. So he prayed two rak'ahs with those next to him then said salam, then those next to him stepped back on their heels and stood in the positions of their companions, and the others came, so he prayed two rak'ahs with them, while the others guarded them, then he said salam. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had four rak'ahs, and the people two rak'ahs each. So on that day Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed the shortening of prayer, and commanded the believers to take arms. So in this hadith is what indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed four with them that day, before Allah revealed to him on shortening prayer what He revealed to him, and that the shortening of prayer Allah the Exalted only commanded him with after that. So the four that day were obligatory upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and those following him, their obligation therein was likewise; for their ruling then in their travel was as their ruling in their residence, and necessarily when that was so, each of these two groups must have made up two rak'ahs each, as they would do were they in residence. So if one says: In this hadith is what indicates the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exited the prayer after finishing the two rak'ahs he prayed with the first group and began prayer at the entry of the second group with him therein; for in the hadith: then he said salam. It is said to him: That salam mentioned in this place may be the salam of tashahhud by which ending the prayer is not intended. And it may be a salam by which he intended informing the first group of the time of its withdrawal. And speech was then permissible for him in prayer, not cutting it, upon what has been reported in that from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, and from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and from Zayd ibn Arqam, upon what we have reported from each of them in the chapter in which we mentioned the aspects of the Dhu al-Yadayn hadith in other than this place of this book. And there has been reported from Jabir ibn Abdillah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed it upon other than this meaning."
"1768","1883 - Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Yazid ibn al-Had narrated to me, he said: Shurahbil ibn Sa'd Abu Sa'd narrated to me, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the fear prayer, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, with a group behind him from behind the group behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sitting, their faces all toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir and both groups said takbir, and he bowed and the group behind him bowed, while the others were sitting, then he prostrated and they prostrated also, while the others were sitting, then he stood and they stood, so they stepped back behind him until they were in the place of their companions, and the other group came, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them one rak'ah and two prostrations, while the others were sitting, then he said salam, so both groups stood and prayed for themselves one rak'ah and two prostrations, one rak'ah and two prostrations each. So this hadith with us is of the impossible which may not be; for in it they entered the prayer while sitting. And the Muslims have agreed that if a man opens prayer sitting then stands and completes it standing, with no excuse in any of that, his prayer is void, so entry is only allowed upon what bowing and prostration will be upon, so it is impossible that those behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the second row entered the prayer while sitting. So there is established from Jabir ibn Abdillah what we reported from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in other than this hadith."
"1769","1884 - And others went in the fear prayer to what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Abu Ayyash al-Zuraqi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr with us at Usfan, while the polytheists were between him and the qiblah, among them, or over them, was Khalid ibn al-Walid, so the polytheists said: They were in a prayer, had we struck them in it it would have been booty. The polytheists said: A prayer will come dearer to them than their fathers and children. He said: So Jibril, upon him peace, came down with the verses between Zuhr and Asr. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Asr, and the people lined in two rows, and he said takbir and they all said takbir with him, then he bowed and they all bowed with him, then he raised and they all raised with him, then he prostrated and the row next to him prostrated, while the rear row stood guarding them with their arms, then he raised and they all raised, then the other row prostrated then raised. And the front row stepped back and the rear row advanced, so he said takbir and they all said takbir with him, then he bowed and they all bowed with him, then he raised and they all raised with him, then he said salam to them, and he prayed it another time in the land of Banu Sulaym."
"1770","1885 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed it, and he mentioned the like of this. And Ibn Abi Layla was of those who went to this hadith. And Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan left it; for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: And let another group come who have not prayed and pray with you. And in this hadith they all prayed together. And in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and in the hadith of Hudhayfah and Zayd ibn Thabit, the entry of the second group in the second rak'ah, they had not prayed before that, so the Quran indicates what the narration from them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought in that, so it was with him worthier than the hadith of Abu Ayyash and Jabir, these two. And Abu Yusuf went to that if the enemy is in the qiblah, the prayer is as Abu Ayyash and Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported. And if they are in other than the qiblah, the prayer is as Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and Hudhayfah and Zayd ibn Thabit reported; for in the hadith of Abu Ayyash they were in the qiblah, and the hadith of Ibn Umar and Hudhayfah and Zayd has nothing of that mentioned, except that there has been reported from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, in that what agrees with what they reported, and he said: The enemy was in other than the qiblah. Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, said: So I validate both hadiths, making the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, and what agreed with it, when the enemy is in other than the qiblah, and the hadith of Abu Ayyash and Jabir when the enemy is in the qiblah. And this is not contrary to the revelation with us, for it is possible His saying: And let another group come who have not prayed and pray with you, is when the enemy is in other than the qiblah. Then Allah revealed to him after that how the ruling of prayer is when they are in the qiblah, so he did both acts together as the two reports came. And this is the soundest view with us in that and worthiest; for validating the reports testifies to it, and there has also indicated that that Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, has been reported from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on the fear prayer, what we have mentioned at the beginning of this chapter of what Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah reported from him, of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying at Dhi Qarad, so that agreed with what Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abdullah ibn Umar and Hudhayfah and Zayd reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that."
"1771","1886 - Then there was reported from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, in that, as his opinion, what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Hashimi Abu Bakr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from al-A'raj, that he heard Abdullah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say on the fear prayer, and he mentioned the like of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in the hadith of Abu Ayyash and the hadith of Jabir ibn Abdillah which agreed with it. So when Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, had known of the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he knew, upon what we reported from him in the hadith of Ubaydullah, and he said: The polytheists were between him and the qiblah, then said this by his opinion: it is impossible they pray thus with the enemy in other than the qiblah, and they pray when the enemy is in the qiblah, as Ubayd reported from him. For if they do not turn their backs to the qiblah with the enemy behind them, it is more likely they do not turn it when they are in front of them. But what we mentioned from him of leaving the turning is when the enemy is in the qiblah. And it may also be likewise when the enemy is also in other than the qiblah, as Ibn Abi Layla said. So our knowledge of his saying encompassed contrary to what Ubaydullah reported from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the enemy is in the qiblah. And he would not have said that except after the abrogation of that was established with him when the enemy is in other than the qiblah, so we made this we reported from him of his saying about the enemy when in the qiblah, and left the ruling of the enemy when in other than the qiblah upon the like of what Ubaydullah reported from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, had once said: The fear prayer is not prayed after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and claimed the people only prayed it with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as they prayed it due to the excellence of prayer with him, and this view with us is nothing; for the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it after him, Hudhayfah prayed it in Tabaristan, and what is in that is too famous to need mentioning here. So if he argues in that by His saying: And when you are among them and establish prayer for them, the verse, and says: He was only commanded with that when among them, so when not among them, what he was commanded of that ceases. It is said to him: He, Mighty and Majestic, said: Take from their wealth charity purifying them and cleansing them thereby, and pray upon them, the verse, so the address here was to him, yet it is agreed that was acted upon after him, as it was acted upon in his life."
"1772","1887 - And Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to me that he heard Abu Abdillah Muhammad ibn Shuja' al-Thalji fault this view of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, and say: Prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), though better than prayer with all the people, yet none may speak therein speech cutting it, so nothing should be done therein which is not done in prayer with others, and what cuts prayer behind others of all impurities cuts it. So when prayer behind him is not cut by going and coming and turning from the qiblah when it is a fear prayer, it is likewise behind others also."
"1773","36 - Chapter: A man in war whom prayer reaches while riding, does he pray or not? 1888 - Ali ibn Ma'bad, he is Ibn Nuh, narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Zirr, from Hudhayfah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say on the day of Khandaq: They distracted us from the Asr prayer. He said: And he did not pray it that day until the sun set. May Allah fill their graves with fire and their hearts with fire and their houses with fire. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to that the rider does not pray the obligation on his mount, even if in a state he cannot dismount, they said: For the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray that day riding. And others opposed them in that and said: If this rider fights, he does not pray, and if the rider does not fight and cannot dismount, he prays, and it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray that day because he was fighting, for fighting is action, and prayer has no action in it. And it is possible he did not pray that day because he had not then been commanded to pray riding. So we examined that."
"1774","1889 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b. (H). 1890 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri, from his father, he said: We were detained on the day of Khandaq until after Maghrib into the night, until when we were sufficed, and that is the saying of Allah the Exalted: And Allah sufficed the believers fighting, and Allah is Strong, Mighty. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Bilal, so he called iqamah for Zuhr, and he perfected its prayer as he used to pray it in its time, then he ordered him and he called iqamah for Asr, so he prayed it likewise, then he ordered him and he called iqamah for Maghrib, so he prayed it likewise, and that was before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed on the fear prayer: On foot or riding. So Abu Sa'id informed that their leaving prayer that day riding was only before that was permitted to them, then it was permitted to them by this verse. So it is established thereby that a man, if in war and unable to dismount from his mount, may pray upon it by gestures. And likewise were a man on the ground, and feared if he prostrates a beast will tear him or a man strike him with a sword, he may pray sitting, if he fears that in standing, and gesture with gestures. And all this is the view of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"1775","37 - Chapter: How is seeking rain, and is there prayer in it or not? 1891 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud, he is Abu Bishr al-Baghdadi, narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Namir, that he heard Anas ibn Malik mention that a man entered the mosque on Friday from a door facing the minbar, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood preaching, so he faced the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing, then said: Messenger of Allah, wealth has perished and roads are cut, so ask Allah to relieve us. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands, then said: O Allah, give us drink. Anas said: By Allah, we saw in the sky no cloud nor wisp, and between us and Sal' no house nor dwelling. He said: So a cloud like a shield rose from behind him, and when it reached the middle of the sky, it spread then rained. He said: By Allah, we saw not the sun for a week. He said: Then a man entered from the door on the next Friday, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood preaching the people, so he faced him standing, then said: Messenger of Allah, wealth has perished and roads are cut, so ask Allah to withhold it from us. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands then said: O Allah, around us and not upon us, O Allah, upon the hills and highlands. He said: So it cleared, and he went out walking in the sun."
"1776","1892 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: It was read to Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth: Your father informed you, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Sharik, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1777","1893 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Zafar Abd al-Salam ibn Mutahhar narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: I was standing at the minbar on Friday, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached, so some of the mosque folk said: Messenger of Allah, rain is withheld, and livestock have perished, so ask Allah to give us drink, so he raised his hands, with no cloud in the sky, so Allah joined the clouds, so it poured upon us until a man would be concerned about himself reaching his family, so we were rained upon seven days. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached on the second Friday; when some of the mosque folk said: Messenger of Allah, houses have collapsed, so ask Allah to lift it from us, he said: So he raised his hands, and said: O Allah, around us and not upon us. So what was above our heads parted, until they were as in a wreath, raining around us while we were not rained upon."
"1778","1894 - Ibn Marzuq and Abu Bakrah narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Humayd, he said: Anas ibn Malik was asked: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raise his hands? He said: It was said to him on a Friday: Messenger of Allah, rain has failed, and the land is barren, and wealth has perished. He said: So he stretched his hands until I saw the whiteness of his armpits, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud."
"1779","1895 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"1780","1896 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt, he said: We said to Ka'b ibn Murrah, or Murrah ibn Ka'b: Narrate to us a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah reward your father, and beware. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against Mudar, so I came to him and said: Messenger of Allah, Allah has aided you and answered you, and your people have perished, so ask Allah for them. So he said: O Allah, give us rescuing, wholesome, fruitful, widespread, heavy, immediate, undelayed rain, beneficial, not harmful. He said: Only a Friday or like it passed until they were rained upon. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to that the sunnah of seeking rain is imploring Allah the Exalted and beseeching Him, as in these reports, and there is no prayer in that, and of those who went to that is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him. And others opposed them in that, of them Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him. So they said: Rather the sunnah in seeking rain is that the imam goes out with the people to the prayer ground and prays there two rak'ahs with them and recites aloud in them, then preaches and turns his cloak, making its top its bottom and its bottom its top, unless it is a heavy cloak he cannot turn so, or it is a taylasan, so he makes its right side upon the left shoulder, and its left side upon the right shoulder. And they said: What was mentioned in these reports of the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his asking thereby, that is also allowed, Allah is asked that, and therein is no rejection that the imam, when he wants to seek rain with the people, do what we mentioned. So we examined what they mentioned of that: Do we find for it evidence in the reports?"
"1781","1897 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdullah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to the prayer ground and sought rain, so he turned his cloak and faced the qiblah."
"1782","1898 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdullah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to the prayer ground and sought rain, so he turned his cloak and faced the qiblah."
"1783","1899 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abbad ibn Tamim informed me, that his uncle, who was of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with the people to the prayer ground to seek rain for them, so he stood and supplicated Allah standing, then turned toward the qiblah and turned his cloak, so they were given rain."
"1784","1900 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi informed us, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and sought rain, so he turned his cloak. He said: I said: He made the top upon the bottom and the bottom upon the top? He said: No, rather he made the left upon the right and the right upon the left."
"1785","1901 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdullah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to seek rain wearing a black khamisah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to take it by its lower part and make it its upper, and when it was heavy for him to turn it, he flipped it upon his shoulder."
"1786","1902 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdullah ibn Zayd: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought rain and turned his cloak. So in these reports is his turning his cloak and the description of turning the cloak how it was, and that he only made what was on his right of it upon his left and what was on his left upon his right when it was heavy for him to make its top its bottom and its bottom its top, so thus we say: what is possible to make its top its bottom and its bottom its top, he turns it so, and what that is not possible in, it is shifted, making the right of it left and the left of it right. So what is in these reports added over what is in the first reports, so it should be acted upon and not left. And:"
"1787","1903 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Hisham, that Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Kinanah of Banu Malik ibn Shurahbil said: My father narrated to me, he said: al-Walid ibn Uqbah sent me to ask for him about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer in seeking rain, so I came to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: We disputed in the mosque about the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer in seeking rain. He said: No, but your nephew al-Walid sent you, while he is the governor of Madinah, and had he sent and asked there would be no harm in that, then Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out humble, lowly, imploring, until he came to the prayer ground, and he did not preach your preaching of this, but he remained in supplication and imploring and takbir, then prayed two rak'ahs as prayed in the two Eids. So his saying: as prayed in the two Eids: may mean he recited aloud in them as recited aloud in the two Eids."
"1788","1904 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and added: So he prayed two rak'ahs with us behind him, reciting aloud in them, without adhan and without iqamah. And he did not say: like the prayer of the two Eids. So that indicated that his saying: like the prayer of the two Eids in the first hadith only meant by it this meaning, that he prayed without adhan nor iqamah, as done in the two Eids."
"1789","1905 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Kinanah, from his father, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Rabi' from Asad. Sufyan said: So I said to the shaykh: Is the sermon before the prayer or after it? He said: I do not know. So in this hadith is mention of prayer and reciting aloud therein, and his reciting aloud therein indicated it is like the Eid prayer done in daytime at a special time, so its ruling is aloud. And likewise the Friday prayer is of daytime prayer, but done on a special day, so its ruling is aloud. So it is established thereby that likewise is the ruling of daytime prayers prayed not on ordinary days, but for an occasion, or on a special day, so their ruling is aloud. And every prayer done on ordinary days in daytime, not for an occasion nor at a special time, its ruling is low recitation. So it is established by what we mentioned that the seeking-rain prayer is an established sunnah not to be left. And that has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from more than one path."
"1790","1906 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Sa'id ibn al-Haytham al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Mabrur, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The people complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of lack of rain, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a minbar, and it was placed in the prayer ground, and he promised the people they would go out one day. Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out when the edge of the sun appeared, so he sat upon the minbar and praised Allah, then said: You have complained to me of the dryness of your region and the delay of rain beyond its time from you, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has commanded you to ask Him and promised you to answer you. Then he said: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, Master of the Day of Judgment, there is no god but Allah, He does what He wills, O Allah, You are Allah, there is no god but You, the Rich, and we are the poor, send down upon us rain, and make what You sent down for us strength and provision to a time. Then he raised his hands and remained raising until the whiteness of his armpits appeared. Then he turned his back to the people, and turned or flipped his cloak while raising his hands. Then he faced the people and descended and prayed two rak'ahs, and Allah produced a cloud, so it thundered and lightened, and rained by Allah the Exalted's permission, so he did not reach his mosque until valleys flowed. So when he saw clothes wrapped upon the people and their hastening to shelter, he laughed until his molars appeared and said: I bear witness that Allah is over all things Capable, and that I am Allah's servant and Messenger."
"1791","1907 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day to seek rain, so he prayed with us two rak'ahs without adhan nor iqamah, he said: Then he preached to us and supplicated Allah and turned his face toward the qiblah, and raised his hands and turned his cloak, making the right upon the left, and the left upon the right."
"1792","1908 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk and Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b. (H). 1909 - And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, who was of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day go out to seek rain, so he turned his back to the people, and faced the qiblah supplicating, then turned his cloak, then prayed two rak'ahs, reciting in them aloud."
"1793","1910 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, and he mentioned the like of it with his chain, except that he did not mention aloud. So in these reports is mention of the sermon with mention of prayer, so it is established thereby that in seeking rain is a sermon, except that they differed on the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sermon when it was. So in the hadith of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Abdullah ibn Zayd: he preached before prayer, and in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he preached after prayer. So we examined that, and found Friday has a sermon, while it is before prayer, and saw the two Eids have a sermon, while it is after prayer, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do. So we wanted to see which of the two sermons the seeking-rain sermon most resembles, to extend its ruling to its ruling. So we saw the Friday sermon is obligatory, and the Friday prayer is bound by it, not sufficing without attaining it, and saw the two-Eid sermon is not so; for the two-Eid prayer suffices also even if he does not preach, and saw the seeking-rain prayer suffices also even if he does not preach. Do you not see that if an imam prayed with the people in seeking rain and did not preach, his prayer would suffice, except that he did wrong in leaving the sermon, so it was more like the ruling of the two-Eid sermon than the ruling of the Friday sermon. So by reasoning its place from the seeking-rain prayer is like its place from the two-Eid prayer, so it is established thereby that it is after prayer, not before it. And this is the view of Abu Yusuf. And that has been reported from one after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed in seeking rain and recited aloud."
"1794","1911 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid went out to seek rain, while he had seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And there went out among those with him al-Bara ibn Azib and Zayd ibn Arqam. Abu Ishaq said: And I was with him that day, so he stood standing upon his mount without a minbar, and sought rain, and sought forgiveness, and prayed two rak'ahs with us behind him, reciting aloud in them, and he did not call adhan that day nor iqamah."
"1795","1912 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr informed us. And he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not mention in his hadith that Abdullah ibn Yazid said: He had seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1796","1913 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid went out to seek rain in Kufah, so he prayed two rak'ahs."
"1797","38 - Chapter: How is the eclipse prayer? 1914 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood and lengthened recitation, then bowed and lengthened bowing, then raised his head and lengthened standing, while shorter than his first standing, then bowed and lengthened bowing, while shorter than his first bowing, then raised his head and prostrated, then stood and did the like of that, except that the first rak'ah of them was longer."
"1798","1915 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1799","1916 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1800","1917 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Urwah and Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
